# =============================================================================
# vks-airgap-cicd — committed source of truth for every operator-tunable value.
#
# HOW TO USE
#   cp .env.example .env      # then edit .env with your air-gap specifics
#   .env is gitignored (per-operator overrides + secrets); this file is not.
#   Both the Makefile (-include .env) and scripts (they source these) read it.
#
# CONVENTIONS
#   - Non-secret values carry a real default here.
#   - `# how:` lines are ACQUISITION COMMANDS. Each is one of:
#       (verified)  we have RUN it, or it comes straight from this repo's own code/targets
#       (inferred)  from a Broadcom page that serves 9.0 content — plausible for 9.1, NOT proven.
#                   Re-check on a real lab. Grade by the URL that actually answers: an explicit
#                   `/9-1/` URL either serves 9.1 (HTTP 200, no redirect) or 404s, so a 200 there
#                   is 9.1; `/latest/` is NOT a safe substitute (measured 2026-08-05: 404 on the
#                   pages this repo cites). Package-reference and vcf-CLI pages exist only at /9-0/.
#     A command we can neither run nor cite is NOT written: the slot says UNVERIFIED and states what
#     IS established (see ARGOCD_KUBECONFIG). A wrong command gets copy-pasted; a missing one gets asked about.
#   - `# where:` lines are UI LOCATIONS, for a value that has no command we can run (a vCenter
#     console path). They are not acquisition commands and carry no provenance tag.
#   - Secrets are COMMENTED placeholders (`<SET-IN-.env>`): set them only in .env
#     so they are never committed and never appear in `git`/`docker inspect`.
#   - Keep this file shell-clean: KEY=value, no spaces around '=', no quotes
#     needed unless the value has spaces. Escape a literal '$' as '$$' for Make.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 0. Run behavior
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: the mirror mode (dual-homed vs sneakernet) is NOT a variable — it's simply WHICH mirror
# commands you run (see README "Two operating modes"): dual-homed → `make mirror`; sneakernet →
# `make mirror-pull && make bundle` (carry the bundle in) then `make bundle-load && make mirror-push`.

# Completion notifications (the terminal bell + best-effort desktop notify that long targets
# like `make mirror` / `make e2e-kind` emit when they finish). Default true. Set false to
# silence them: `make e2e-kind NOTIFY=false`, or uncomment `NOTIFY=false` here / in .env.
# COMMENTED on purpose (runtime toggle): an uncommented value would be re-sourced by load_env
# and clobber a `make … NOTIFY=false` override. Unset ⇒ the code default (true) applies.
# The bell is tty-gated and desktop-notify needs a display, so both are already no-ops in CI.
# NOTIFY=true

# Which OCI engine builds/pushes the offline Maven builder image. (The mirror itself uses crane — a
# static binary, no engine.)
#
# DEFAULT: podman. `make deps` installs it. DO NOTHING — leave this commented.
#
# DOCKER IS SUPPORTED — opt in, and you run TWO commands, not a hand-typed CA install:
#
#   make deps CONTAINER_ENGINE=docker    # installs docker + its rootless prerequisites
#   make trust-harbor                    # wires the Harbor CA for YOUR engine and PROVES it with a
#                                        # real login handshake (it does not just place a file)
#
#   make engine-check                    # read-only: which engine, which mode, and what it COSTS you
#
# What it costs (measured, not asserted):
#   podman            no sudo, ever      (daemonless; the CA goes in per command)
#   docker ROOTLESS   no sudo            (the daemon reads ~/.config/docker/certs.d/<host>/ca.crt)
#   docker ROOTFUL    ONE SUDO PER REGISTRY — /etc/docker/certs.d is root-owned, and the `docker`
#                     group grants SOCKET access, not write access to /etc. It cannot be engineered
#                     away, only disclosed. `make trust-harbor` prints the exact line to run.
#
# Rootless docker is available from the distro on Photon and Ubuntu 26.04+, but NOT on Ubuntu 24.04
# (its docker.io ships no rootless helper). We will not add a third-party apt repo to your jump box,
# so docker there is rootful-only — `make deps` says so out loud.
#
# Not supported with docker: HARBOR_INSECURE=1 (plain HTTP). That is podman-only — a CA drop-in never
# enables plain HTTP; docker would need an insecure-registries entry plus a daemon reload.
# `make e2e-kind` needs docker regardless of this setting — that is kind, not us.
#
# LEAVE IT COMMENTED. It has a code fallback, so an uncommented value here is re-sourced by
# load_env's `set -a` AFTER make has put your command line in the environment — it would PIN the
# engine, defeating both the podman-first auto-detection and any per-run override. Unset is the
# correct state: podman if present, else docker.
# CONTAINER_ENGINE=podman

# SHOW_SECRETS — force `make creds-show` to print passwords even when stdout is NOT a terminal.
# LEAVE IT COMMENTED. It is read from the environment BEFORE this file is loaded, so a value set
# here is IGNORED BY DESIGN: `load_env` sources this file with `set -a`, and one uncommented line
# would otherwise re-arm the credential leak for every redirect, CI capture and walk log, silently
# (an absent mask produces no output to notice). Pass it per-invocation instead:
#     SHOW_SECRETS=1 make creds-show
# SHOW_SECRETS=0

# CREDS_NO_PROBE — forbid every CLUSTER READ `make creds-show` would otherwise do (today: the
# guest-node SSH password, read from a <cluster>-ssh-password secret in the vSphere Namespace).
# LEAVE IT COMMENTED, for the same reason as SHOW_SECRETS above: it is snapshotted from the
# environment BEFORE this file is loaded, so a value set here is IGNORED BY DESIGN — and an
# uncommented line would be exported by `load_env`'s `set -a` and silently defeat a per-run
# override, which is the clobber class check-env-clobber.sh exists to catch.
# It is set automatically by scripts/test-creds-show.sh so the OFFLINE suite — which runs inside
# `static-check` and `make ci`, and whose fixtures carry REAL lab IPs — can never dial a live lab.
# You want it by hand only when reporting on a checkout whose kubeconfig points somewhere you would
# rather not touch. Pass it per-invocation:
#     CREDS_NO_PROBE=1 make creds-show
# CREDS_NO_PROBE=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Harbor (registry) — real lab: installed as a Supervisor Service (Part A),
#    then DISCOVERED; KinD: auto-installed + auto-written to .env.kind.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# stage: discover (after Part A Harbor install)  [auto in KinD (.env.kind)]
# how: after Harbor install, kubectl get svc -n <harbor-namespace> -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}' (or the FQDN you set)
# ⚠️ BARE HOST — no scheme, no trailing slash (like SUPERVISOR_HOST). harbor.sh builds
#    "${SCHEME}://${HARBOR_URL}/api/v2.0/…", so a leading https:// yields https://https://… and
#    `curl: (6) Could not resolve host: https`. MEASURED 2026-08-04: fetch-ca.sh TOLERATES a scheme
#    (it parses host/port), so §6 passes and §7 is where it bites — two steps from the mistake.
#    harbor_setup() now strips a scheme and warns, but set it correctly here.
# Commented (like HARBOR_USERNAME, B14) so its ~18 `:?` guards are NON-vacuous: load_env's `set -a`
# would otherwise export this placeholder, and a `:?` proves only "set", never "reachable". KinD
# auto-publishes the real LB IP to .env.state (06-install-harbor state_sets it BEFORE any consumer);
# a real lab operator sets it in .env. Unset → the `:?` fires with guidance (better than a
# non-resolving `harbor.vks.local`). `harbor.vks.local` is NEVER a working Harbor address here (Harbor
# uses its own LB IP; the FQDN-via-/etc/hosts variant was superseded — see kind-tls-fidelity.md).
# HARBOR_URL=<SET-IN-.env>
# Project for mirrored CI/CD + build-base infra images (Tekton, Gitea, Kaniko, temurin...)
HARBOR_INFRA_PROJECT=cicd
# Project for the built application image.
#
# TENANT NOTE: set this EQUAL to HARBOR_INFRA_PROJECT if you are a Harbor project-admin rather than a
# system-admin. Only a system-admin can create a robot spanning TWO projects, and two robots cannot
# substitute: the kaniko pod carries ONE registry credential and must PULL its builder/runtime images
# from HARBOR_INFRA_PROJECT and PUSH the app image to HARBOR_APP_PROJECT with that same credential.
# The repo names do not collide (infra: <app>-builder, golang, eclipse-temurin, ...; app: bare <app>),
# so one project holds both safely. Do the collapse in `.env` — these lines are UNCOMMENTED here, so
# load_env's `set -a` would clobber a `make ... HARBOR_APP_PROJECT=...` override.
HARBOR_APP_PROJECT=apps
# Create auto-created Harbor projects PUBLIC (so kubelet CAN pull anonymously).
# COMMENTED so `make mirror HARBOR_PUBLIC_PROJECTS=false` / a .env override survives (load_env
# would otherwise clobber it). Unset ⇒ scripts default to true. Set false for a private-project
# lab: the app-namespace image-pull Secret (harbor-pull) is created for you by `make gitops`, in
# EVERY app namespace, and every app's Deployment already references it — nothing to do by hand.
# HARBOR_PUBLIC_PROJECTS=true
# Path to the Harbor CA cert (self-signed). Used for crane/kaniko/podman trust.
# PREFER OBTAINING THIS OUT OF BAND — a file the platform team hands you is strictly better than
# anything `make fetch-harbor-ca` can derive from the wire (no transcription, no partial compare).
HARBOR_CA_FILE=./secrets/harbor-ca.crt

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HARBOR_SERVICE_NAMESPACE — the ESCAPE HATCH for a tenant who cannot list namespaces.
# Normally NOT set: `make harbor-admin-password` and `make harbor-ca-from-cluster` DISCOVER the
# Harbor service namespace by label (appplatform.vmware.com/serviceId=harbor). A locked-down tenant
# may be FORBIDDEN to list namespaces cluster-wide — the scripts now say so precisely instead of
# claiming Harbor is absent, but they still cannot proceed. Set this and they skip the probe.
# COMMENTED on purpose: load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an uncommented value would be
# exported and would DISABLE discovery on every box, including the ones where it works.
# stage: request (only if the label probe reports FORBIDDEN)
# how: ask your platform admin — or, if you can list namespaces:
#      kubectl get ns -l appplatform.vmware.com/serviceId=harbor -o name
# HARBOR_SERVICE_NAMESPACE=

# KUBECTL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT — how long a single kubectl probe may hang before failing.
# Unset ⇒ the scripts default to 15s. 23-argocd-preflight.sh records that an UNBOUNDED kubectl
# hangs for over two minutes against a black-hole endpoint instead of failing, which turns a fast
# diagnosis into a stall. Raise it only for a genuinely slow Supervisor.
# COMMENTED on purpose: it is a per-run tunable, and an uncommented value here would be exported by
# load_env and would silently override `make <target> KUBECTL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=…`.
# stage: none (a tunable, never required)
# how: not applicable — you choose it
# KUBECTL_REQUEST_TIMEOUT=15s
# Expected SHA-256 of the Harbor CA, obtained OUT OF BAND (colon-hex or bare hex, either is accepted).
# This is the ONLY thing that makes `make fetch-harbor-ca` an authentication rather than a
# trust-on-first-use: that command takes the CA off the very connection it is meant to protect, and a
# MITM's chain is self-consistent by construction. MEASURED 2026-08-05: without a pin, an evil
# self-signed cert was accepted and reported as "VERIFIED". With no pin and no terminal, the fetch now
# REFUSES; with a terminal it prints the digest and asks. Set it per run when you have it:
#   make fetch-harbor-ca HARBOR_CA_SHA256=<digest>
# how: request it from whoever operates Harbor, over a channel that is NOT this connection (a call, a
#      ticket, a signed mail). If they hand you the CA FILE instead — which is strictly better — compute
#      it yourself:  openssl x509 -in <their-ca.crt> -noout -fingerprint -sha256
# ⚠️ There is deliberately NO command that fetches this from the endpoint. Any such command would read
#      the digest over the connection it is supposed to authenticate, which is the defect itself.
# ⚠️ LEFT COMMENTED DELIBERATELY. It is a per-run value, and `load_env` sources this file with `set -a`
# AFTER make has placed a command-line override in the environment — an uncommented value here would
# clobber that override (this repo has check-env-clobber for exactly that class).
# HARBOR_CA_SHA256=
# Registry transport. 0 (default) = HTTPS with a trusted self-signed CA (mimics VCF/VKS
# 9.1's self-signed Harbor); 1 = plain HTTP (KinD fast-iteration only). The KinD flow
# (06-install-harbor.sh) serves HTTPS on the Harbor LoadBalancer IP (cert SAN=IP) and
# publishes HARBOR_URL/HARBOR_CA_FILE to .env.kind; the CA is trusted sudo-free via
# SSL_CERT_FILE (crane) / --cert-dir (podman) / containerd certs.d / an in-cluster ConfigMap.
# Left COMMENTED (not `=0`) on purpose: this is a RUNTIME toggle, and load_env sources this
# file with `set -a`, so an uncommented value here would CLOBBER a `make e2e-kind
# HARBOR_INSECURE=1` override. Unset ⇒ the scripts default to 0 (secure). Flip it per-run
# (`make e2e-kind HARBOR_INSECURE=1`) or persist it by adding `HARBOR_INSECURE=1` to .env.
# HARBOR_INSECURE=0

# ENGINE_SKIP_CA_RED — skip the "-CA RED" in `make engine-trust-check` / the jump-box matrix.
#
# By default that check tries to log in to Harbor with NO CA wired and REQUIRES it to FAIL. Without it,
# the "CA method" column is an assertion rather than a measurement: docker MERGES certs.d with the host
# SYSTEM STORE, so on a box where the CA is already trusted (an operator who ran update-ca-certificates,
# or a certs.d entry left over from an earlier run) the whole CA step is a NO-OP that still prints a
# confident method — a leg that measures nothing and reports success.
#
# Set to 1 ONLY when you knowingly run against a registry whose CA is already in your system trust store
# and the RED therefore cannot go red. It makes the run WEAKER; it is not a convenience flag.
#
# COMMENTED on purpose: load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an uncommented value would CLOBBER a
# per-run `make engine-trust-check ENGINE_SKIP_CA_RED=1` (and, worse, silently disable the check for
# everyone). Unset ⇒ the RED runs.
# ENGINE_SKIP_CA_RED=0

# SKIP_DOTENV — ignore `.env` entirely (this file and .env.kind are still read).
# The KinD e2e sets it (E2E_SKIP_DOTENV ?= 1 in the Makefile) so a local run reproduces a
# FRESH box: the you-choose secrets do not exist and must be GENERATED, exactly as they are
# for a new operator or a CI runner. Without it, a local run silently reads YOUR .env and the
# fresh-box path is never exercised.
# Set it yourself only to sanity-check a script against a clean environment:
#   how: SKIP_DOTENV=1 make <target>          (or: make e2e-kind E2E_SKIP_DOTENV=0 to use your .env)
# COMMENTED on purpose — a runtime toggle (see HARBOR_INSECURE above).
# SKIP_DOTENV=0
# Robot/admin account used to push mirrored images + let the pipeline push builds.
# stage: after Part A install
# how: Scenario 1 (you installed Harbor): `admin`. Scenario 2 (tenant): your robot's login name
#      `robot$<name>` from secrets/harbor-robot.env (`make harbor-robot`, needs Harbor project-admin).
# COMMENTED, like HARBOR_PASSWORD below: an uncommented value here is exported by load_env's `set -a`,
# so a tenant who sets ONLY HARBOR_PASSWORD in .env would silently push as `admin` (401). The KinD
# flow gets `admin` from 05-kind-up (state_set, like the generated password), not from this line.
# Never read HARBOR_USERNAME/HARBOR_PASSWORD make-side (`$(HARBOR_USERNAME)`): a `robot$…` value has a
# `$` that `make -include .env` misparses (B31) — the scripts read them bash-side, where it is correct.
# ⚠️ SINGLE-QUOTE IT when you set it in .env: `HARBOR_USERNAME='robot$vks-cicd'`. load_env sources
# .env with `set -a`, so an UNQUOTED robot$vks-cicd has `$vks` expanded away -> `robot-cicd` -> 401
# against Harbor. `make harbor-robot` writes the pair already quoted; check-doc-robot-quoting gates
# it repo-wide. (Carried here 2026-08-21 from docs/detailed-steps.md, which was retired as a stale
# duplicate of scenario-1 - this was the one thing in it that lived nowhere else.)
# HARBOR_USERNAME=<SET-IN-.env>
# how: `make harbor-robot` writes secrets/harbor-robot.env (needs Harbor project-admin) — copy the
#      password from there. If you do NOT hold project-admin: request the robot from your platform
#      admin (Scenario 2). If YOU installed Harbor: `make install-harbor-service` GENERATED it and
#      published it to .env.state — you did not choose it, and it is NOT Harbor12345 (that 401s).
# how: Harbor already installed, and you have no local copy? read it off the Supervisor:
#      NS=$(kubectl get ns -o name | grep -oE 'svc-harbor-[a-z0-9]+' | head -1)
#      kubectl -n "$NS" get secret harbor-core-ver-1 -o jsonpath='{.data.HARBOR_ADMIN_PASSWORD}' | base64 -d
#      VERIFIED 2026-08-11 on a live 9.1 lab: admin + that value -> HTTP 200 at /api/v2.0/users/current.
# HARBOR_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>
# Name for the least-privilege CI robot account created by `make harbor-robot` (push+pull on the
# two mirror projects). The robot lands as `robot$<name>`; its secret is written to
# secrets/harbor-robot.env, AND publishes HARBOR_USERNAME/HARBOR_PASSWORD to .env itself.
HARBOR_ROBOT_NAME=vks-cicd

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Gitea (self-hosted Git) — WE INSTALL on VKS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
GITEA_NAMESPACE=gitea
# External URL (humans + webhook registration UI).
# GITEA_URL — DERIVED from GITEA_HOST (`http://${GITEA_HOST}`) by 40-install-gitea.sh and creds.sh.
# COMMENTED so GITEA_HOST stays the single source of truth for the hostname; an uncommented value
# here is exported by load_env and would pin a URL that silently disagrees with the ingress route.
# stage: you choose (optional — set it ONLY if the scheme/port differ from the ingress route)
# GITEA_URL=http://gitea.vks.local
# In-cluster URL used by TEKTON (clone + tag write-back) — resolves via kube-DNS to the Gitea
# Service. Tekton runs in this cluster, so this is correct for it. It is NOT correct for an ArgoCD
# that runs somewhere else — see GITEA_ARGOCD_URL_OVERRIDE below.
GITEA_INTERNAL_URL=http://gitea-http.gitea.svc:3000
#
# The Gitea URL ARGOCD'S repo-server clones from. It must be routable FROM THE CLUSTER ARGOCD RUNS
# IN. On a real lab ArgoCD is a Supervisor Service — a different cluster — so:
#   * gitea-http.gitea.svc:3000  resolves only INSIDE the guest cluster        -> unusable
#   * http://gitea.vks.local     exists only in YOUR /etc/hosts, in no DNS     -> unusable
#     (and dialling the ingress LB IP sends `Host: <ip>`, which matches no vhost -> 404, not a clone)
# So Gitea gets its OWN LoadBalancer (like Harbor and ArgoCD each already have) and this points at
# it. COMMENTED because 40-install-gitea.sh DISCOVERS it and publishes it; an uncommented value here
# would be exported by load_env and clobber the discovered one (and the in-cluster fallback).
# stage: discover (after `make install-gitea`)  [auto in KinD via .env.kind]
# how: kubectl -n $GITEA_NAMESPACE get svc gitea-http -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}'
# NOTE the distinct name: `make gitops` RESOLVES this address from Gitea's live LoadBalancer Service
# at the moment it needs it. 40-install-gitea.sh used to PUBLISH a GITEA_ARGOCD_URL that gitops read
# back as an input — but an auto-published value is ALWAYS set on the next run, so a STALE address is
# indistinguishable from a deliberate override (the same trap as INGRESS_LB_IP_OVERRIDE). Set this
# ONLY if Gitea's own LB address is not reachable from the cluster ArgoCD runs in.
# GITEA_ARGOCD_URL_OVERRIDE=http://<gitea-lb-ip>:3000
#
# Discovered by 40-install-gitea.sh and published to .env.kind. Commented for the same reason.
# GITEA_LB_IP=<gitea-lb-ip>
#
# Gitea's Service type. Code default: LoadBalancer (an off-cluster ArgoCD must reach it; a
# LoadBalancer keeps its ClusterIP so Tekton is unaffected). Set to ClusterIP only if you know
# ArgoCD runs in THIS cluster and you cannot spare a LoadBalancer address. Commented: it has a code
# default and is a per-run override.
# GITEA_SERVICE_TYPE=LoadBalancer
# How long 40-install-gitea.sh waits for that LoadBalancer to get an address.
# GITEA_LB_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180

# Seconds `ca_verifies_endpoint` (scripts/lib/tls.sh) waits when checking that a pinned CA actually
# verifies an endpoint, before a credential is submitted to it. COMMENTED on purpose: it is a
# per-run knob, and an uncommented value here is sourced with `set -a` AFTER make has put a
# `make X CA_VERIFY_TIMEOUT=...` override into the environment, which would silently defeat it.
# Raise it only for a genuinely slow endpoint. ⚠️ A TIMEOUT is reported as UNREACHABLE (rc 2), never
# as a bad anchor — conflating those tells an operator whose lab is DOWN that their certificate is
# wrong, which is the failure this whole check exists to stop.
# choose: a whole number of seconds; 15 suits every lab measured so far.
# CA_VERIFY_TIMEOUT=15

# Port `make creds` probes to decide whether the recorded INGRESS_LB_IP is actually ANSWERING.
# COMMENTED on purpose (a per-run knob; an uncommented value is sourced with `set -a` AFTER make has
# placed an override in the environment, silently defeating it).
# WHY THE PROBE EXISTS: INGRESS_LB_IP is published to the state overlay by an install and SURVIVES A
# REBUILD. MEASURED 2026-08-05 — the report printed an /etc/hosts line and four http://*.vks.local
# URLs for 192.168.101.135 from a lab that no longer existed (no ping, tcp/80 closed, no such
# LoadBalancer). The probe can only DOWNGRADE the claim, and it is biased AGAINST a false "dead":
# anything other than a refused/timed-out connect counts as alive.
# choose: the port your ingress actually listens on; 80 suits every lab measured so far.
# INGRESS_PROBE_PORT=80
# acquire: n/a (reserved — Gitea is served over HTTP; no code reads this today)
# GITEA_CA_FILE=./secrets/gitea-ca.crt   # reserved — Gitea is served over HTTP (no TLS); no code reads it today
GITEA_ORG=demo
# Are the <app>-deploy repos PRIVATE? They are seeded PUBLIC (50-seed-gitea-repos.sh), so ArgoCD
# clones them anonymously and needs NO credential. Set true only if you make them private — then
# `make gitops` registers a repo credential with ArgoCD (and a TENANT needs the argocd CLI for it,
# since only a PROJECT-scoped repo credential is theirs to create).
# Commented: it has a code default and is a per-run override.
# GITEA_DEPLOY_PRIVATE=false
# NO GITEA_APP_REPO / GITEA_DEPLOY_REPO here on purpose. With more than one app they are PER-APP
# (<app>-app / <app>-deploy, derived from apps/registry.tsv by scripts/lib/apps.sh). A global value
# would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and CLOBBER the loop's per-app export — every app would
# be seeded into javawebapp's repos. That is the .env.example clobber class (check-env-clobber).
# Gitea's container image. Defaults (in code) to the ref mirrored into Harbor — the air-gap path.
# Overridden only by a test that has NO Harbor (e2e-cross-cluster exercises the ArgoCD topology, not
# the air gap). Commented: it has a code default and is a per-run override.
# GITEA_IMAGE=<harbor>/<project>/gitea/gitea:<tag>
# ⚠️ 'admin' is NOT reserved by Gitea — MEASURED 2026-08-05 against a live instance: creating a
# user named 'admin' via the admin API returned HTTP 201 (the probe user was deleted again).
# 'gitea_admin' is only Gitea's own documented default, not a constraint. Owner chose 'admin'.
# ⚠️ This takes effect on a FRESH install only — it does not rename an existing admin account.
# ⚠️ COUPLED with GITEA_ADMIN_EMAIL below (~line 809). Gitea enforces UNIQUE EMAILS, so a create
# with a new username but the SAME email is rejected on the EMAIL — change one, change both.
# MEASURED 2026-08-11: this pair 532 lines apart is why a rename shipped and broke seed-gitea.
GITEA_ADMIN_USER=admin
# stage: you choose (login for the component we install / KinD only)
# GITEA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>
# Persistent volume size for Gitea data (SQLite DB + repos).
GITEA_STORAGE_SIZE=5Gi
# CI user Tekton/ArgoCD use to clone + push (write-back). A PAT is minted at seed time.
GITEA_CI_USER=ci-bot
# stage: you choose (login for the component we install / KinD only)
# GITEA_CI_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>
# GITEA_CI_TOKEN=<SET-IN-.env — minted by scripts/50-seed-gitea-repos.sh>

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Tekton — WE INSTALL on VKS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
TEKTON_NAMESPACE=tekton-pipelines
# Namespace where the pipeline, triggers, and EventListener run.
CI_NAMESPACE=ci

# `make builder-probe` — proves the CARRIED builder image is RUNNABLE, not merely fetchable (B3).
# It runs one throwaway pod that executes `mvn -o -v` inside the image, so it catches a mangled
# CONFIG blob (entrypoint/user/PATH) that mirror-verify's blob checks cannot see.
# when you need it: it runs automatically on the sneakernet air-gap leg. Run it by hand after a
#   `make builder-push` if you want the runnability answer without waiting for `make verify`.
# LEFT COMMENTED DELIBERATELY: the code reads it as ${BUILDER_PROBE_NAMESPACE:-${CI_NAMESPACE:-ci}},
#   i.e. it FALLS BACK to CI_NAMESPACE. load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an UNCOMMENTED
#   value here would be exported and would silently DEFEAT that fallback — an operator who changed
#   only CI_NAMESPACE would still get the probe in `ci`. Uncomment it only to put the probe in a
#   namespace genuinely DIFFERENT from CI_NAMESPACE.
# BUILDER_PROBE_NAMESPACE=<defaults to CI_NAMESPACE>
# How long to wait for the probe pod to reach Running. Raise it on a slow node or a cold image pull.
# COMMENTED for the same reason as its sibling directly above, and for the same reason as every key
# in section 8: uncommented, load_env's `set -a` exported it and BEAT `24-builder-probe.sh:36`'s
# `${BUILDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT:-180s}` — measured, an override of 999s arrived as 180s. It sits outside
# section 8, which is exactly why B122's 13-key enumeration could not see it; a name-shaped
# predicate over the whole file found it. 180s is the code-side default, so nothing changes for
# anyone who sets nothing.
# how: make builder-image BUILDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT=600s
# BUILDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT=180s

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. ArgoCD (GitOps CD) — PROVIDED by VKS
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TOPOLOGY (real lab, confirmed against Broadcom 9.1 "Install the Argo CD Operator/Instance"):
# ArgoCD is a Supervisor Service — the operator + argocd-server run ON THE SUPERVISOR, in a vSphere
# Namespace (e.g. argocd-instance-1), NOT in the guest/workload cluster. So to deploy javawebapp INTO the guest
# cluster (VKS_CLUSTER_NAME), that cluster must be REGISTERED as an ArgoCD destination (`argocd cluster add`,
# or the argocd-attach-service CRs) and the Application's destination pointed at it. The committed Application
# (k8s/argocd/application.yaml) uses the IN-CLUSTER destination (https://kubernetes.default.svc) — correct for
# KinD (one cluster: ArgoCD's own == the workload cluster), but on a real Supervisor-hosted ArgoCD that
# resolves to the SUPERVISOR, not the guest cluster. `make argocd-preflight` reports `ArgoCD is OFF-CLUSTER`
# for exactly this. ARGOCD_NAMESPACE = the k8s namespace the ArgoCD instance watches Applications in
# (real lab: the vSphere Namespace the instance runs in, e.g. argocd-instance-1; KinD: argocd).
# COMMENTED, and load-bearing. scripts/e2e-cross-cluster.sh passes ARGOCD_NAMESPACE as an ENV-PREFIX
# override (ARGOCD_NAMESPACE="$ARGOCD_NS" ... 70-configure-argocd.sh). load_env sources this file with
# `set -a` AFTER that override is in the environment, so an UNCOMMENTED value here wins and the
# override is SILENTLY INERT. It only looked fine because the value happened to equal the default.
# A code default applies when it is unset in EXACTLY ONE consumer -- 08-install-argocd-service.sh
# falls back to VKS_NAMESPACE. Three others have NO fallback and die on it:
# 31-fetch-argocd-kubeconfig.sh, 99-verify.sh, 98-uninstall-all.sh. So an operator MUST set it in
# .env (uncommented HERE would clobber the override above -- that is why this line stays commented).
# ARGOCD_NAMESPACE=argocd
# stage: discover (after Part A ArgoCD install)  [auto in KinD (.env.kind) as ARGOCD_LB_IP]
# how (verified command shape; the NAMESPACE `argocd-instance-1` is inferred from 9.0 docs — confirm on your lab):
#      kubectl get svc -n <argocd-vsphere-namespace> argocd-server -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}'
# COMMENTED, and load-bearing. This SELECTS which ArgoCD server the CLI talks to. Uncommented, every
# argocd-CLI call dials a hostname NOTHING in this repo creates — so the TENANT (api) path silently
# falls back to `request` and applies NOTHING while reporting success. `make env-populate` discovers
# it; `make creds-show` prints it.
#
# ⚠️ USE THE COMMAND-LINE FORM FOR A PER-RUN OVERRIDE:
#     make gitops ARGOCD_SERVER=<a-name-the-cert-carries>          e.g. argocd-server
# NOT the env-prefix form (`ARGOCD_SERVER=<name> make gitops`), which this line used to recommend.
# ⚠️ AND NOT AN LB IP -- this example said `<lb-ip>` until 2026-08-17, which is the wrong VALUE in
# the one place a reader COPIES from. The ⚠️ block ~14 lines below explains why an IP can never
# verify, but a correction printed below the copyable command does not retract the command: the
# reader pastes the example and only meets the explanation afterwards, if at all. Measured on a
# real lab the same day: the IP form fails hostname verification even with the correct CA.
# MEASURED 2026-08-05: the Makefile's `-include .env` makes a FILE-defined make variable, and a
# file-defined variable BEATS an environment one (GNU make default). So the env-prefix form works on a
# fresh box — and SILENTLY STOPS WORKING after `make env-populate`, because that writes the discovered
# ARGOCD_SERVER into .env (02-env.sh:95). Measured with .env carrying 192.168.101.131:
#     ARGOCD_SERVER=1.2.3.4 make <target>   ->  the recipe sees 192.168.101.131
# No error; it just uses the stale discovered value instead of the one you typed. A command-line
# assignment outranks both, so it is the form that always works.
#
# ⚠️ AND THE OLD REASON WAS THE WRONG MECHANISM. It blamed `load_env`'s `set -a`, but ARGOCD_SERVER is
# in load_env's snapshot-protect list — load_env preserves an explicit override, it does not clobber it.
# The clobber happens ONE LEVEL UP, in make, before any script runs, which is why the snapshot cannot
# help: it faithfully preserves the value make already replaced.
# ⚠️ A NAME the cert carries, NOT the LB IP. argocd-server's default cert has DNS SANs only and no IP
# SAN, so an IP fails hostname verification even with the correct CA — and the name fault MASKS the
# anchor fault, so you cannot see a wrong CA until the name is right. Upstream mints 'argocd-server'
# and 'localhost' regardless of namespace -- but ONLY while it generates its own certificate
# [community: argo-cd docs/operator-manual/tls; a tls.crt supplied in argocd-secret or
# argocd-server-tls REPLACES every name]. So ASK your platform team first; this is the
# fallback guess. Map the name to the LB IP in /etc/hosts.
# ✅ LAB-VERIFIED 2026-08-17 on a real cut (this line previously said UNVERIFIED and told you to go
# read the SANs; that has now been done, so do not re-derive it). argocd-server at the lab's LB:
#     subject == issuer == 'O = Argo CD'  (SELF-SIGNED)
#     subjectAltName = DNS:localhost, DNS:argocd-server, DNS:argocd-server.cicd,
#                      DNS:argocd-server.cicd.svc, DNS:argocd-server.cicd.svc.cluster.local
#     -> NO IP SAN, exactly as predicted above.
# The three arms, measured against that real server rather than a synthetic certificate:
#     the LB IP  + the correct CA  -> curl (60) "no alternative certificate subject name matches
#                                     target host name '<lb-ip>'"        <- an IP can NEVER verify
#     'argocd-server' + the same CA -> rc=0, HTTP 200                     <- this is the working form
#     'argocd-server' + NO CA       -> curl (60) "self-signed certificate" <- the anchor IS required
# So both names this comment recommends ('argocd-server', 'localhost') really are present, and
# ARGOCD_CA_FILE really is required rather than optional. NOTE the namespace component was 'cicd'
# on that cut -- it is YOUR ARGOCD_NAMESPACE, so do not copy the five names as constants.
# ARGOCD_SERVER=<SET-a-name-the-cert-carries>
# Path to the ArgoCD server's self-signed CA cert. Fetch it with `make fetch-argocd-ca`
# (from ARGOCD_LB_IP in KinD, or the lab's argocd-server LoadBalancer IP).
# REQUIRED for any VERIFYING path: with the address right but no anchor the CLI still fails
# 'signed by unknown authority'. Leave unset ONLY if ArgoCD presents a PUBLICLY-TRUSTED
# certificate (then there is no CA to fetch), or if you accept --insecure, which turns
# verification OFF rather than achieving it -- and the write path does not.
# how: make fetch-argocd-ca   (fetches the ArgoCD server's self-signed CA from its endpoint)
# ARGOCD_CA_FILE=./secrets/argocd-ca.crt
# Extra GLOBAL flags for every `argocd` invocation. The CLI reads this variable itself, so it is the
# one lever that reaches can-i, `app create`, `app wait` and anything added later.
#   You normally leave this ALONE: when ARGOCD_CA_FILE is set and non-empty, the scripts APPEND
#   `--server-crt <that file>` here for you (argocd_tls_opts, scripts/lib/os.sh). Setting it
#   yourself is for the other flags — e.g. --grpc-web behind a proxy that cannot do HTTP/2.
#   ⚠️ COMMENTED deliberately. An uncommented value here is sourced with `set -a` AFTER the ambient
#   environment, so it would CLOBBER a per-run `make X ARGOCD_OPTS=...` override AND defeat the
#   append above. If you set it, you own TLS: `--insecure` disables verification entirely, which is
#   how a trust-anchor failure came to be reported to operators as a permissions denial.
# how: leave unset unless you need a non-TLS flag; run `argocd --help` for the global flag list.
# ARGOCD_OPTS=--grpc-web
# Expected SHA-256 of the ArgoCD CA, obtained OUT OF BAND. Same contract as HARBOR_CA_SHA256 above —
# see that entry for why an unpinned fetch is trust-on-first-use rather than authentication.
#   make fetch-argocd-ca ARGOCD_CA_SHA256=<digest>
# how: request it from whoever operates ArgoCD, over a channel that is NOT this connection. If they hand
#      you the CA file: openssl x509 -in <their-ca.crt> -noout -fingerprint -sha256
# ⚠️ Left COMMENTED for the same clobber reason.
# ARGOCD_CA_SHA256=
# NO ARGOCD_APP_NAME / ARGOCD_DEST_NAMESPACE here on purpose: one ArgoCD Application PER APP, each
# deploying into its OWN namespace (named after the app). Both are derived from apps/registry.tsv.
# A global value here would clobber the per-app export (see the note on the Gitea repos above).
# API server the Application deploys to. Commented ⇒ code default https://kubernetes.default.svc
# (IN-CLUSTER: KinD, or ArgoCD-in-guest). On a real lab where ArgoCD runs on the SUPERVISOR, set this
# to the GUEST cluster's API URL so ArgoCD deploys THERE, not on the Supervisor — `make argocd-register-guest`
# registers the guest cluster and sets it for you (admin path; a tenant requests registration — see §4 topology).
# Commented (not =…) so the in-cluster default applies for KinD and a real-lab value / register step isn't clobbered.
# ARGOCD_DEST_SERVER=https://kubernetes.default.svc
# The AppProject each Application belongs to. Code default: `default`, which permits every
# destination and every repo — correct for KinD and for Scenario 1 (you installed ArgoCD, so you are
# its admin). A TENANT (Scenario 2) is given their OWN AppProject, and an Application whose project
# does not permit its destination + its repo is REJECTED. So set this to your AppProject, and ask
# your platform admin to add — per app — the app's namespace to `spec.destinations` and the
# <app>-deploy repo URL to `spec.sourceRepos`. (It used to be hardcoded in the manifest AND absent
# from the envsubst allowlist, so it could not be changed even by editing the file.)
# Commented: it has a code default and is a per-run override.
# how: kubectl -n $ARGOCD_NAMESPACE get appprojects.argoproj.io
# ARGOCD_PROJECT=default
# How long `make gitops` waits for ArgoCD to actually FETCH a revision from the deploy repo before
# it fails. This is the gate that proves ArgoCD's repo-server can reach GITEA_ARGOCD_URL — a wrong
# URL used to sync green forever (an Application ArgoCD never reconciled has no error to report).
# ARGOCD_REPO_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180
# Branch in <app>-deploy that ArgoCD tracks (must be a branch, not a tag/commit,
# so the Tekton write-back commit is picked up automatically).
ARGOCD_TRACK_BRANCH=main
# ArgoCD UI/API transport in KinD. 0 (default) = upstream self-signed TLS on 443,
# reached by the ArgoCD LoadBalancer IP with `--insecure` (mimics VCF/VKS 9.1's
# self-signed ArgoCD); 1 = server.insecure plain HTTP (KinD fast-iteration only).
# 07-install-argocd.sh exposes argocd-server on its OWN LoadBalancer (not the ingress,
# matching VKS) and publishes ARGOCD_LB_IP to .env.kind.
# Left COMMENTED (not `=0`) for the same reason as HARBOR_INSECURE above: a runtime toggle
# an uncommented value would clobber. Unset ⇒ default 0 (secure). Flip per-run
# (`make e2e-kind ARGOCD_INSECURE=1`) or persist by adding `ARGOCD_INSECURE=1` to .env.
# ARGOCD_INSECURE=0

# How long `make argocd-auth-check` waits for argocd-server's /api/v1/session to answer before it
# calls the endpoint unreachable. The check exists to separate "your credential is wrong" from
# "argocd-server never received the request", so a too-short timeout would report the SECOND as the
# FIRST — the exact confusion the target was built to end. Unset ⇒ default 20.
# Left COMMENTED for the clobber rule: load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER make has put a
# per-run value in the environment, so an uncommented line silently defeats
# `make argocd-auth-check ARGOCD_SESSION_TIMEOUT=60` on a slow lab.
# ARGOCD_SESSION_TIMEOUT=20

# The scheme argocd-auth-check dials. It exists ONLY so the offline test can point the check at a
# plain-HTTP stub; on any real lab it must stay https, and there is no operator reason to change it.
# Left COMMENTED for the SAME clobber reason, and here it is load-bearing rather than tidy: an
# uncommented `https` would be sourced over the test's own override and the offline test could never
# reach its stub. Unset ⇒ default https.
# ARGOCD_SESSION_SCHEME=https

# How long argocd-auth-check waits for argocd-server's LoadBalancer to actually SERVE before it
# authenticates. This is NOT belt-and-braces: `make argocd-address` returns the INSTANT
# .status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip appears, and cloud-provider-kind assigns that IP long before its
# Envoy is routing (the K1.5 race). Certification row 1 failed here in 0s — the shape of a refused
# connection, not of a credential or trust fault (backlog B163). The wait is NON-FATAL: if the
# endpoint never answers, the check SAYS so and carries on, because "it is not serving" is itself the
# diagnosis. 30 x (a 3s probe + a 2s sleep) ~= 150s worst case — NOT 60s: the per-attempt curl --max-time counts too, and the sleep runs after the final attempt (measured: attempts=3 interval=2 -> 15s, not 6s, against an unroutable address). Both COMMENTED for the usual clobber reason — an uncommented value
# here is sourced AFTER a per-run override and would silently defeat it.
# how: make argocd-auth-check ARGOCD_READY_ATTEMPTS=60
# ARGOCD_READY_ATTEMPTS=30
# how: make argocd-auth-check ARGOCD_READY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=5
# ARGOCD_READY_INTERVAL_SECONDS=2

# Where argocd_session_token leaves curl's rc and the HTTP status for the caller to read. It is a
# FILE and not a shell global on purpose: callers use `tok="$(argocd_session_token ...)"`, and a
# command substitution is a SUBSHELL, so a global assigned inside it can never reach the caller —
# while the token itself must keep stdout to itself or it would be folded into the return value.
# Exists mainly so the offline test can point it somewhere hermetic; there is no operator reason to
# set it. COMMENTED for the same clobber reason as ARGOCD_SESSION_SCHEME above, and here it matters
# for the same test-override reason. Unset => ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.argocd-session-diag.<uid>
# how: ARGOCD_SESSION_DIAG=/tmp/mydiag make argocd-auth-check
# ARGOCD_SESSION_DIAG=
#
# ARGOCD_PW_DIAG — where argocd_admin_password records HOW it obtained the password
#   (source=secret|env|none, plus rc and reason). A FILE and not a variable because the
#   caller uses pw="$(argocd_admin_password ...)", which is a SUBSHELL a global cannot
#   escape. The auth-check reads it so a 401 is only blamed on the credential when a
#   credential was actually READ — an unreadable Secret otherwise yields a non-empty
#   garbage value and the check would exonerate the address and the anchor it never tested.
#   Default: ${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/.argocd-pw-diag.$(id -u), written under umask 077.
#   COMMENTED on purpose: load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an uncommented
#   value would be exported and clobber a per-run override.
# how: ARGOCD_PW_DIAG=/tmp/mypwdiag make argocd-auth-check
# ARGOCD_PW_DIAG=

# --- Cross-cluster ArgoCD: registering the GUEST cluster as a destination ------------------
# WHEN you need this: ArgoCD runs in a DIFFERENT cluster from where javawebapp deploys. On a real VKS
# lab that is the normal case — ArgoCD is a Supervisor Service running ON THE SUPERVISOR, while the
# app must land in the GUEST/workload cluster. The committed Application uses the in-cluster
# destination (correct for KinD, where they are the same cluster), which on a Supervisor-hosted
# ArgoCD would resolve to the SUPERVISOR. `make argocd-preflight` reports `ArgoCD is OFF-CLUSTER` vs `IN the workload cluster`.
#
# HOW it happens: set ARGOCD_KUBECONFIG below and run `make gitops` (or `make install-all`) —
# it AUTO-INVOKES `make argocd-register-guest` first. You never run kubectl by hand.
#
# What registration does (scripts/71-argocd-register-guest.sh, all declarative kubectl):
#   guest cluster : creates the `argocd-manager` ServiceAccount + a cluster-admin binding + a
#                   NON-EXPIRING service-account-token Secret;
#   ArgoCD cluster: writes ArgoCD's `Cluster` Secret (argocd.argoproj.io/secret-type: cluster)
#                   carrying the guest API URL + CA + that bearer token;
#   then publishes ARGOCD_DEST_SERVER so the Application targets the guest.
# It does NOT install a second ArgoCD in the guest, and it deliberately does NOT use
# `argocd cluster add`: that stores an EXPIRING credential when the source kubeconfig authenticates
# with an x509 client cert (argoproj/argo-cd#13175) — which is exactly what
# `vcf cluster kubeconfig get` hands you — so the registered cluster would later flip to Unknown.
#
# ADMIN-ONLY: minting the argocd-manager cluster-admin RBAC needs cluster-admin on the guest, and
# ArgoCD's `clusters` is a global RBAC resource. A pure VKS tenant REQUESTS this from the platform
# team; they cannot self-service it.
#
# Path to the kubeconfig of the cluster ARGOCD RUNS IN. Leave UNSET when ArgoCD and the workload
# share a cluster (KinD, and any single-cluster deploy) — registration is then skipped entirely.
#
# On a real lab ArgoCD is a Supervisor Service, so this is a kubeconfig for the SUPERVISOR (scoped to
# the vSphere Namespace the ArgoCD instance runs in, e.g. argocd-instance-1) — NOT the workload
# cluster. `vcf cluster kubeconfig get` fetches the WORKLOAD cluster's kubeconfig; that is KUBECONFIG.
#
#   how (INFERRED — Broadcom 9.0-via-redirect docs; we have never run it on a lab):
#      make fetch-argocd-kubeconfig
#   which runs, per Broadcom ("Connect to the Supervisor as a vCenter SSO User" + "VCF CLI Context,
#   Architecture, and Configuration"): a Supervisor context is created with
#   `vcf context create --endpoint https://$SUPERVISOR_HOST --username <u>@$VKS_SSO_DOMAIN ...`, the
#   VCF CLI "respects the KUBECONFIG environment variable for writing to alternate locations", so we
#   point KUBECONFIG at this file; creating a Supervisor context also auto-creates the per-vSphere-
#   Namespace contexts (`<ctx>` and `<ctx>:<namespace>`), and we select `<ctx>:$ARGOCD_NAMESPACE`.
#   The target then PROVES it works (argocd-server must be visible) rather than trusting the file.
#   It is INTERACTIVE (the CLI prompts for the password; a password on argv is forbidden).
# ARGOCD_KUBECONFIG=./secrets/argocd.kubeconfig
#
# Name of the VCF CLI context created for the Supervisor by `make fetch-argocd-kubeconfig`.
# ARGOCD_SUPERVISOR_CONTEXT=argocd-supervisor
#
# The Supervisor's CA certificate (preferred over skipping TLS verification).
#   how: ask your vSphere admin, or download it from the Supervisor endpoint.
# VKS_CA_CERT_FILE=./secrets/supervisor-ca.crt
# OPTIONAL cross-check: the expected SHA-256 of the CA in VKS_CA_CERT_FILE. When set, `make vks-login`
# refuses if the file's digest does not match — BEFORE the vCenter credential is submitted. Set it only
# if the platform team gave you a digest as well as the file; a file obtained out of band is already the
# correct path, so leaving this unset is not a downgrade and produces no warning.
# how: request it from the platform team over a channel that is NOT this connection, or compute it from
#      the file they gave you: openssl x509 -in <their-ca.crt> -noout -fingerprint -sha256
# ⚠️ There is deliberately no command that reads this from the endpoint — that would authenticate the
#      connection against itself, which is the defect this exists to close.
# ⚠️ Left COMMENTED: per-run value, and load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER a command-line
#      override is placed in the environment (check-env-clobber guards that class).
# VKS_CA_SHA256=
#
#
# The guest API URL ArgoCD will DIAL. It must be routable FROM THE ARGOCD CLUSTER — the address in
# your kubeconfig may be reachable from your laptop but not from the Supervisor. Defaults to the
# guest kubeconfig's server. Set it when they differ (e.g. the guest control-plane VIP).
#   how: kubectl --kubeconfig $GUEST_KUBECONFIG config view --minify -o jsonpath='{.clusters[0].cluster.server}'
# GUEST_API_SERVER=https://<guest-api-vip>:6443
#
# The ServiceAccount minted on the guest for ArgoCD to act through (+ its namespace).
# ARGOCD_MANAGER_SA=argocd-manager
# ARGOCD_MANAGER_NS=kube-system
#
# Skip TLS verification of the GUEST API when registering. Only for a stand-in whose API cert has
# no SAN for the address ArgoCD dials (the two-KinD e2e). Never on a real lab.
#
# ⚠️ SETTING THIS IN `.env` DOES NOTHING — BY DESIGN. It is honoured ONLY as a per-run prefix:
#         ARGOCD_REGISTER_INSECURE=1 make gitops
# Unlike every other toggle here, this choice does not end with the run: it is written into the
# ArgoCD Cluster Secret, so EVERY later sync to that destination dials the guest API with TLS
# verification off. A line in `.env` would therefore be a standing, invisible downgrade — and a
# ratchet, because `load_env` sources this file with `set -a` AFTER your environment, so a per-run
# `=0` could not have undone it. `71-argocd-register-guest.sh` snapshots the value BEFORE `load_env`
# and warns if it saw a file-sourced 1, so the ignore is loud rather than silent.
# ARGOCD_REGISTER_INSECURE=0
#
# --- The TENANT write path: argocd-server, not kubectl ---------------------------------------
# On a real lab the ArgoCD instance lives in an ADMIN-owned vSphere Namespace, so a tenant usually
# CANNOT `kubectl apply` an Application there. But in ArgoCD, `applications` and `repositories` are
# PROJECT-scoped RBAC resources — so an AppProject role lets you create them through **argocd-server**
# with no Kubernetes RBAC at all. That is the mechanism a tenant is actually granted.
#
# Set these two to use it (and to let `make argocd-preflight` TELL you whether it is open to you —
# the single most useful thing to learn before a 20-minute mirror). The token never reaches argv.
#   how: argocd login <server> --sso        (interactive), then:
#   how: argocd account generate-token --account <you>      -> paste the value into .env
# ARGOCD_AUTH_TOKEN=<SET-IN-.env>
#
# HOW the Application gets written. Commented: it has a code default and is a per-run override.
#   auto    (default) — MEASURE what is open to you and pick: kubectl, else the argocd API, else request.
#   kubectl           — write straight into the ArgoCD namespace (Scenario 1 / KinD; needs k8s RBAC there).
#   api               — write through argocd-server (the TENANT path: an AppProject role, no k8s RBAC).
#                       Needs ARGOCD_SERVER + ARGOCD_AUTH_TOKEN + the argocd CLI.
#   request           — write NOTHING. Render each Application to ./out/ and print exactly what to ask
#                       your platform team for. The give-up path.
# ARGOCD_MECHANISM=auto
#
# Whether registering the guest cluster as an ArgoCD destination should be attempted.
#   auto (default) — only if ArgoCD is off-cluster AND the guest is not registered AND you may do it.
#   never          — a TENANT: registration is the platform team's job. Skip it silently.
#   force          — do it anyway.
# ARGOCD_REGISTER=auto
#
# The name your platform team registered your guest cluster under. A tenant usually CANNOT list the
# ArgoCD cluster Secrets (they live in the admin's namespace), so the NAME may be all you have —
# ArgoCD's Application.destination accepts `name:` as well as `server:`.
# ARGOCD_DEST_CLUSTER_NAME=vks-guest

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4b. Ingress (WE install it) — ONE LoadBalancer fronting the browser UIs at
#     *.vks.local so operators reach Gitea/ArgoCD/the app by hostname instead of
#     `kubectl port-forward`. Air-gap honest: hostnames resolve via /etc/hosts on
#     the jump box/clients -> the ingress LB IP (NO internet DNS / sslip.io).
#     Harbor keeps its OWN direct LoadBalancer (its LB IP is load-bearing for the
#     containerd insecure-registry pull path — see 06-install-harbor.sh).
#
#     Pluggable: INGRESS_CONTROLLER selects the implementation.
#       istio           (default) — WE install the Istio control plane + ingress gateway
#                                   (images from Harbor via the helm hub override).
#       istio-existing            — the platform team ALREADY installed Istio; we install
#                                   NOTHING and only attach our routes to their gateway.
#                                   Run `make istio-preflight` first — it discovers the mesh
#                                   and tells you exactly what (if anything) to request from
#                                   the mesh admin.
#       traefik                   — the lighter single-binary Traefik.
#     All expose the SAME *.vks.local hostnames behind one LB IP.
#
#   ⚠️ THIS VALUE IS PUBLISHED TO THE STATE OVERLAY once you install. `make install-ingress
#   INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio-existing` (scenario-1 §11) state_sets it into .env.state, which
#   load_env sources LAST — so from then on it is the effective value for every later command,
#   which is what you want.
#
#   ⚠️ IT IS A SELECTOR (it names WHICH ingress implementation), so it is SNAPSHOT-PROTECTED by
#   load_env: an EXPLICIT `INGRESS_CONTROLLER=<x> make …` is captured before any file is sourced
#   and restored afterwards, so a per-run override still wins over the overlay. Leave it UNSET to
#   get the published value.
#   MEASURED 2026-08-04 — before that protection existed, running §11 in `istio-existing` mode
#   permanently reddened `make static-check`: test-gateway-image pins `INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio` as
#   a command-scoped prefix, the overlay overwrote it, and the classifier's self-tests then ran in
#   the foreign-mesh mode where provenance CANNOT be asserted (6 of 7 cases wanted rc=1, got 0).
#   A supported operator action must not be able to break the pre-commit gate.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio
# Ingress hostnames — the UIs the ingress fronts. GITEA_HOST is the SINGLE source of truth for
# Gitea's hostname: GITEA_URL derives from it (see §3) rather than repeating it.
#
# There is deliberately NO ARGOCD_HOST: ArgoCD is NOT routed through the ingress (neither is
# Harbor). Each keeps its OWN LoadBalancer — Harbor's LB IP is load-bearing for the containerd
# registry-pull path, and ArgoCD's own self-signed-TLS LB mirrors the VKS lab. Reach ArgoCD at
# ARGOCD_SERVER / ARGOCD_LB_IP, not at a *.vks.local name. (An ARGOCD_HOST=argocd.vks.local
# knob used to sit here with ZERO consumers in the entire repo — it promised a route that does
# not exist.)
GITEA_HOST=gitea.vks.local
# APPS_REGISTRY — the app registry the loops read (apps/registry.tsv): one row per demo app.
# COMMENTED: the code reads it as `${APPS_REGISTRY:-${REPO_ROOT}/apps/registry.tsv}` (a DYNAMIC
# fallback), and an uncommented value here would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and pin it,
# defeating the fallback. Override only to point the loops at a different registry (e.g. a test one).
# stage: you choose (optional)
# APPS_REGISTRY=./apps/registry.tsv

# APP_DOMAIN — the DNS suffix every app's ingress hostname is DERIVED from: an app in
# apps/registry.tsv is reachable at <app>.${APP_DOMAIN} (app_host(), scripts/lib/apps.sh).
# There is deliberately NO per-app <APP>_HOST variable here: that made "adding an app is ONE ROW"
# false (a new row died until you also added a line to THIS file) and no gate could see the second
# edit. One global instead — which is also what lets a shared Istio Gateway admit every app at once
# with a single `*.${APP_DOMAIN}` entry. Only the domain is operator-tunable; the <app> part IS the
# registry row.
#
# ⚠️ `.local` IS RESERVED FOR mDNS (RFC 6762 §3), AND THAT IS DELIBERATE HERE, NOT AN OVERSIGHT.
# A `.local` name is not resolvable through ordinary DNS on a host running Avahi/Bonjour — some
# resolvers refuse it outright. This design never asks it to be: `show-dns-records.sh` emits real-DNS
# A records for **Harbor and ArgoCD only**, and scenario-1 says in as many words that "the
# `*.vks.local` names are `/etc/hosts`-only". Nothing crosses that boundary — 70-configure-argocd.sh
# resolves ArgoCD from the live Service, 23-argocd-preflight.sh BLOCKS a cluster-local override, both
# Gateways are plain HTTP, and Harbor's certificate carries an IP SAN. So the reservation bites
# exactly one case: putting these UI hostnames into REAL DNS.
#
# ⚠️ AND IF YOU DO CHANGE IT, IT DOES NOT MOVE EVERYTHING — measured. `GITEA_HOST`,
# `TEKTON_DASHBOARD_HOST` and `GITEA_ADMIN_EMAIL` below are hardcoded to `vks.local`, so setting
# `APP_DOMAIN=vks.test` moves the APP hosts and leaves gitea+tekton behind: two domains, two
# `/etc/hosts` lines, and **no gate asserts a shared suffix**. The escape hatch is to change all of
# them together, by hand, in this file. Deriving them here (`GITEA_HOST=gitea.${APP_DOMAIN}`) was
# tried and REVERTED: the derivation expands AT SOURCE TIME, so an operator's `.env` arrives too
# late and a command-line `APP_DOMAIN=` is clobbered by this very file — measured, `GITEA_HOST` still
# resolved to `gitea.vks.local`. A knob that LOOKS functional is worse than an honest literal.
APP_DOMAIN=vks.local
TEKTON_DASHBOARD_HOST=tekton.vks.local
# --- Istio: WE install it (INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio) ---
# ISTIO_VERSION must match images/images.txt istio/pilot + istio/proxyv2 tags;
# Renovate bumps it in lockstep with them (Docker images group), and
# check-image-alignment is the deterministic backstop.
# renovate: datasource=docker depName=istio/pilot
ISTIO_VERSION=1.30.3

# The Kubernetes Gateway API CRDs. NOTHING ELSE INSTALLS THEM — and that was a real bug:
# on KinD they appear only because cloud-provider-kind force-installs its own, so our
# gateway-api e2e leg was green because of a KinD-only shim, and the path we advertise as
# verified could simply not exist on a real VKS cluster (Istio does NOT ship them either).
# We now install the STANDARD channel ourselves when they are absent and we own the cluster.
#
# THE PIN IS NOT FREE — IT MUST BE THE VERSION THE PINNED ISTIO VENDORS.
# Istio ships a Gateway API Go client compiled against ONE version. CRDs NEWER than that client
# re-introduce the supportedFeatures []string -> []object skew that crash-loops controllers; OLDER
# ones can be refused by the safe-upgrades admission policy. DERIVE it, never guess:
#
#   curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/istio/istio/release-<ISTIO_MINOR>/go.mod | grep gateway-api
#
# Istio 1.30 vendors sigs.k8s.io/gateway-api v1.5.1 → that is the pin.
# (Renovate solo-bumped this to v1.6.0 — NEWER than Istio's own client — within MINUTES of it being
# added, despite the comment saying not to. That is why the two are now GROUPED in renovate.json:
# a comment is not a control.)
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=kubernetes-sigs/gateway-api
GATEWAY_API_VERSION=v1.5.1
ISTIO_NAMESPACE=istio-system
#
# --- Istio: SOMEONE ELSE installed it (INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio-existing) ---
#
# WHICH ROUTE API. Left COMMENTED = auto: prefer the Kubernetes Gateway API whenever Istio is an
# accepted GatewayClass, else fall back to the classic Gateway/VirtualService API.
#   gateway-api  what Broadcom's VKS walkthrough uses (`gatewayClassName: istio`). Needs NOTHING
#                from the mesh admin: we create a Gateway in our own namespace and Istio
#                auto-provisions the proxy AND its LoadBalancer (the proxy inherits istiod's
#                image hub, so on an air-gapped cluster it pulls from Harbor automatically).
#                This also works when the VKS Istio package's shared ingress gateway is OFF —
#                which is its DEFAULT (istio.gateways.ingress.enabled=false).
#   classic      Gateway + VirtualService bound to an EXISTING shared gateway workload. Requires
#                that gateway to exist, plus its selector label (discovered) and usually rights
#                in its namespace.
#   how: kubectl get gatewayclass istio -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions[?(@.type=="Accepted")].status}'
# ISTIO_ROUTE_API=auto
#
# Gateway-API only: the namespace holding the Gateway we create (Istio provisions
# `<ISTIO_GATEWAY_NAME>-istio` — the proxy Deployment + its LoadBalancer Service — right here).
# ISTIO_GWAPI_NAMESPACE=vks-ingress
#
# Gateway-API only: the GatewayClass to attach to. `istio` is Istio's own.
# ISTIO_GATEWAY_CLASS=istio
#
# ALL of these stay COMMENTED: they are DISCOVERED from the live cluster by
# scripts/lib/istio.sh. Setting one here would be sourced into the environment and
# would SKIP discovery for that value — pinning us to our own install's naming and
# hiding the platform team's real gateway. Uncomment one only to OVERRIDE discovery
# (e.g. you have no RBAC to read the gateway namespace and the mesh admin gave you
# the values, or the cluster has more than one ingress gateway and you must choose).
#
# Namespace of the ingress-gateway Service.
#   how: kubectl get svc -A -o json | jq -r '.items[]|select(any(.spec.ports[]?;.port==15021))|select(.spec.selector.istio!=null)|.metadata.namespace'
#   (port 15021 is the istio-proxy status-port — it identifies a GATEWAY. istiod does
#    NOT expose it, so this will not accidentally match the control plane.)
# ISTIO_GATEWAY_NAMESPACE=istio-ingress
#
# Name of that Service.
#   how: kubectl get svc -A -o json | jq -r '.items[]|select(any(.spec.ports[]?;.port==15021))|select(.spec.selector.istio!=null)|.metadata.name'
# ISTIO_GATEWAY_SERVICE=istio-ingressgateway
#
# The VALUE of the gateway's `istio:` selector label. THE load-bearing value: a Gateway
# must select the gateway workload with exactly `istio: <this>`. The istio/gateway helm
# chart derives it from the HELM RELEASE NAME, so on a mesh you did not install it is
# very unlikely to be `ingressgateway`. A Gateway with a non-matching selector is accepted
# by the API server with NO error and binds nothing -> connection refused, not a 404.
#   how: kubectl -n <ns> get svc <svc> -o jsonpath='{.spec.selector.istio}'
# ISTIO_GATEWAY_LABEL=ingressgateway
#
# Name of the Gateway resource WE create (when we are allowed to create one).
# ISTIO_GATEWAY_NAME=vks-uis
#
# Set ONLY when the platform team owns a shared Gateway that you may reference but not
# create: we then create NO Gateway and only VirtualServices (in our own namespaces).
# Its servers[].hosts must admit our three UI hosts (exactly, or via a *.vks.local
# wildcard) — `make istio-preflight` asserts this instead of letting the routes
# silently never match.
#   how: ask the mesh admin; then `kubectl -n <ns> get gateway <name> -o jsonpath='{.spec.servers[*].hosts}'`
# ISTIO_SHARED_GATEWAY=istio-ingress/shared-gw
#
# HOW we install Istio when INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio: `helm` (DEFAULT) or `package`.
# ⚠️ The default is HELM, and this file said `package` from the commit that introduced it
#    (98276c1) until 2026-08-26. The code has always read `${ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD:-helm}`
#    (44-install-ingress.sh:45, 96-verify-gateway-image.sh:64); see 44's header for the three
#    measured reasons. This mattered: the two methods install DIFFERENT Istio minors and are
#    covered by DIFFERENT provenance guards, so a reader who believed `package` was the default
#    believed 43's air-gap check had protected them when in fact helm ran under a different gate.
#   package — a VKS Standard Package. A Carvel PackageInstall CR that the guest cluster's own
#             kapp-controller reconciles: no helm, no vcf CLI, no imgpkg. Ships Istio
#             1.28.5+vmware.1-vks.1 (the VMware-certified build).
#   helm    — helm install of istio/base + istiod + gateway, images pulled from OUR Harbor via
#             global.hub. Ships 1.30.3 — TWO MINORS NEWER than the package.
# Neither is wrong; the package is the VKS-native path but NOT the default, and its downgrade is
# accepted deliberately (BACKLOG.md B476). Override per run:
#     make install-ingress ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD=package
# LEFT COMMENTED ON PURPOSE. load_env sources this file with `set -a`, AFTER make has put a per-run
# override in the environment -- so an uncommented value here would silently CLOBBER the override
# and the run would install the other flavour while reporting the one you asked for. Same reason
# every other per-run toggle in this file is commented; `make check-env-clobber` enforces it.
# ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD=package
#
# Which version of the Istio PACKAGE to install (ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD=package only). Unset = the
# NEWEST the guest cluster offers; `make list-vks-packages` prints them. An unoffered value is
# REFUSED with the real list rather than failing later at reconcile.
# "Newest" is a TRUE VERSION sort -- `sort_by(vkey)`, the shared key in lib/os.sh, built precisely
# because jq's bare `sort` is lexicographic and would rank "1.100" below "1.9". (An earlier note
# here described the lexicographic behaviour; the code no longer does that, and a doc advertising a
# defect that has been fixed sends the next reader to the wrong mechanism.)
# ⚠️ WHEN "newest" is decided moved on 2026-08-26: `make install-ingress ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD=package`
# now resolves it in the PRE-FLIGHT and PINS the install to it, so the air-gap check and the install
# provably judge the same Package. MEASURED the same day: istio ships EIGHT Package objects, one per
# version, and the count GROWS -- a repository reconcile between two independent lookups was enough
# to make them disagree.
# ISTIO_PACKAGE_VERSION=1.28.5+vmware.1-vks.1
#
# The package's refName. Only change it if Broadcom renames the package; `make list-vks-packages`
# prints what this cluster offers. Commented for the same clobber reason as the two above.
# ISTIO_PACKAGE_NAME=istio.kubernetes.vmware.com
#
# Proceed with ISTIO_INSTALL_METHOD=package even when the package's images resolve from a registry
# that is NOT local or mirrored. Set it ONLY on a lab that genuinely has internet, and know what it
# costs: the install will SUCCEED and prove NOTHING about the air gap.
# WHY THE CHECK EXISTS. The package path has no `global.hub`; kbld resolves the bundle's ImagesLock
# RELATIVE TO THE BUNDLE, so images come from wherever the Package CR's imgpkgBundle lives.
# MEASURED 2026-08-25 on a 9.1 guest cluster: istio.kubernetes.vmware.com resolves to
# projects.packages.broadcom.com/..., while the cluster-BOOTSTRAP packages (antrea, pinniped, ...)
# resolve to localhost:5000. Two different mechanisms -- `localhost:5000` is NOT a property packages
# inherit; the Supervisor relocated THAT repo at cluster creation. `make mirror` cannot stage these
# (digest-addressed inside a vendor bundle repo, and images/images.txt carries the HELM versions),
# so relocating the standard-packages repo is a PLATFORM-TEAM action (imgpkg copy).
# ISTIO_PACKAGE_ALLOW_REMOTE=0
#
# Set ONLY if the gateway is NOT a LoadBalancer (or is fronted by something else) and you were
# given the address that reaches it. Normally the address is RESOLVED from the live gateway.
#
# NOTE the distinct name: INGRESS_LB_IP itself is OUR PUBLISHED STATE (every install/attach writes
# the address it resolved into .env.kind), so it is always set after a previous run and can never
# be trusted as an input — consuming it once made a Gateway-API attach report the PREVIOUS classic
# gateway's IP while the route check passed through stale classic routes (a false green). The
# override therefore has its own variable, which nothing auto-publishes.
# INGRESS_LB_IP_OVERRIDE=10.0.0.42
# --- Traefik (INGRESS_CONTROLLER=traefik) ---
TRAEFIK_NAMESPACE=traefik

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4c. Pod Security Admission (PSA) — REQUIRED for a real VKS guest cluster
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A VKS guest cluster runs the PSA controller and ENFORCES the `restricted` Pod Security
# Standard BY DEFAULT from VKS/TKr v1.26 — "pods violating security are rejected unless
# namespace configuration is changed" (Broadcom, "Configure PSA for VKr 1.25 and Later").
# Only kube-system / tkg-system / vmware-system-cloud-provider are exempt; every namespace
# WE create is not. KinD enforces nothing, so a stack that installs cleanly locally can have
# its pods REJECTED outright on a real lab — invisibly.
#
# So we label each namespace we create with the MINIMUM level its workloads actually need.
# These defaults were MEASURED against a live cluster, not guessed — re-derive them any time
# with `make psa-check` (it uses a server-side dry-run label, so the API server evaluates the
# real running pods and reports the violations; it changes nothing).
#
# Values: restricted | baseline | privileged | none (none = do not label; for a cluster whose
# platform team manages PSA centrally, e.g. via the podSecurityStandard ClusterClass variable).
#
# gitea / tekton-pipelines / javawebapp are restricted-clean as they ship.
PSA_LEVEL_GITEA=restricted
PSA_LEVEL_TEKTON=restricted
PSA_LEVEL_APP=restricted
# The CI namespace runs the build TaskRuns. KANIKO BUILDS AS ROOT (runAsUser=0, unrestricted
# capabilities, no seccompProfile), so `restricted` REJECTS them. baseline is genuinely required
# here — it is not a workaround. (A restricted-only platform would need a rootless builder.)
PSA_LEVEL_CI=baseline
# The namespace holding our Gateway. The proxy Istio AUTO-PROVISIONS there sets no
# seccompProfile, so `restricted` rejects it — and that pod is created by the platform's istiod,
# NOT by us, so we cannot make it compliant. baseline is required.
PSA_LEVEL_INGRESS=baseline
# istio-system, when WE install the mesh (Scenario 1): istiod sets no seccompProfile.
# On a real lab the platform owns this namespace and this value is unused.
PSA_LEVEL_ISTIO_SYSTEM=baseline
# The Traefik controller's namespace (INGRESS_CONTROLLER=traefik). Deliberately NOT
# PSA_LEVEL_INGRESS: that var's `baseline` exists for the ISTIO gwapi namespace's
# auto-provisioned proxy, and reusing it here would loosen traefik for an unrelated reason.
# `restricted` is what traefik's own securityContext already satisfies (non-root, uid 65532,
# seccompProfile RuntimeDefault, caps drop ALL, read-only rootfs, binds :8000 not :80 so it needs
# no NET_BIND_SERVICE). That was reasoned, not measured — `make psa-check` now carries a NS_SPEC
# row for it and measures it with a server-side dry-run.
PSA_LEVEL_TRAEFIK=restricted

# --- Additional operator knobs (surfaced by `make check-env-coverage`) ---------------------
# Harbor's helm chart repository. Point these at an INTERNAL chart mirror for a fully air-gapped
# install (the chart is fetched on the internet side; the IMAGES come from Harbor either way).
HARBOR_CHART_REPO_NAME=harbor
HARBOR_CHART_REPO_URL=https://helm.goharbor.io
# Where the ArgoCD install manifest is cached between runs (avoids re-downloading it).
# COMMENTED: the code derives it as `${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/vks-airgap-cicd-manifests`
# (07-install-argocd.sh:72). Pinning /tmp here would export it and make TMPDIR ignored.
# stage: you choose (optional — a fixed cache dir)
# ARGOCD_MANIFEST_CACHE=/tmp/vks-airgap-cicd-manifests
# Namespace the Istio CONTROL PLANE runs in. COMMENTED on purpose: in istio-existing mode it is
# DISCOVERED (`kubectl get deploy -A -l app=istiod`). Uncomment ONLY to override discovery — e.g.
# you lack RBAC to list deployments cluster-wide and the mesh admin told you the namespace.
#   how (verified): kubectl get deploy -A -l app=istiod -o jsonpath='{.items[0].metadata.namespace}'
# ISTIOD_NAMESPACE=istio-system
# Sneakernet bundle output (see `make bundle` / `make bundle-load`).
#
# BUNDLE_OUT_DIR — where `make bundle` WRITES the tarball. COMMENTED on purpose, and it must stay
# that way: load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an uncommented value is exported and
# CLOBBERS a `make bundle BUNDLE_OUT_DIR=<dir>` override. It used to say `./bundle`, which broke
# `make e2e-sneakernet` outright — that target passes a transfer dir, the clobber sent the tarball
# INTO ./bundle (the directory tar is archiving), and tar failed with "file changed as we read it".
# Unset ⇒ the code default (the repo root); 11-bundle.sh also now REFUSES to write inside BUNDLE_DIR.
# stage: you choose (optional — a per-run destination, e.g. a USB mount)
# BUNDLE_OUT_DIR=/media/usb
# BUNDLE_TARBALL — the carried tarball that `make bundle-load` unpacks on the air-gap box.
# COMMENTED, for the same reason as BUNDLE_OUT_DIR above: 20-bundle-load.sh requires it
# (`${BUNDLE_TARBALL:?}`) and every caller passes it per-run — `make bundle-load
# BUNDLE_TARBALL=/path/to/vks-airgap-cicd-bundle-*.tar.zst`, and jumpbox-run.sh does the same for
# the sneakernet e2e. An uncommented value here is exported by load_env's `set -a` and CLOBBERS
# that override, so bundle-load looked for ./bundle.tar.gz and died `bundle not found` while the
# real tarball sat in the transfer dir. There is no sensible default: the filename is timestamped.
# stage: you choose (the path of the tarball you actually carried across)
# BUNDLE_TARBALL=/media/usb/vks-airgap-cicd-bundle-20260712-184355.tar
# BUNDLE_COMPRESSOR — how `make bundle` compresses the archive: none (default) | gzip | zstd.
# COMMENTED, same clobber reason as the two above: it is a per-run choice
# (`make bundle BUNDLE_COMPRESSOR=gzip`), and an uncommented value here is exported by load_env's
# `set -a` and would silently defeat that override.
#
# WHY THE DEFAULT IS `none` (a plain .tar), which looks wrong and is not:
#   * COMPRESSION BUYS ~1%. The payload is already-compressed OCI layer blobs, so gzip measured 99.2%
#     of raw on the real cache — while costing MINUTES of single-threaded CPU on an 11 GB bundle.
#   * THE COMPRESSOR IS A CROSS-BOX CONTRACT. The INTERNET box picks it; the AIR-GAP box must decode
#     it — and cannot install anything if it can't. Photon's tar is TOYBOX, which has NO `--zstd`
#     option AT ALL (installing the zstd binary does not help), and `zstd` ships on neither base
#     image. A plain .tar needs no compressor binary and no tar flag: it works on toybox, busybox,
#     GNU and bsdtar alike. It deletes the whole failure class instead of relocating it.
# Set gzip/zstd only if you know the box on the far side can decode it (bundle-load probes tar's real
# capability and refuses with an actionable message rather than dying inside tar).
# stage: you choose (optional — leave unset unless you have a reason)
# BUNDLE_COMPRESSOR=none
# BUNDLE_TOOLS_FORCE — replace the air-gap box's OWN kubectl/helm/jq/yq with the ones the bundle carried.
# COMMENTED (per-run override; an uncommented value here would be exported by load_env and silently win).
# Default (unset): `make bundle-load` KEEPS a working tool the box already has — your lab's kubectl is
# probably pinned to your cluster's version, and shadowing it from ~/.local/bin would be a version-skew
# bug we handed you. It still REPLACES one that is too old to work (helm 2; a kubectl more than one minor
# from the carried one). Set this to 1 when the box's copy is broken or the wrong arch.
# stage: you choose (optional)
# BUNDLE_TOOLS_FORCE=0
# BUNDLE_SKIP_STATIC_CHECK — skip the "is the binary we are carrying statically linked?" check in
# `make bundle`. COMMENTED, and you almost certainly do not want it: a dynamically-linked binary carried
# across the gap fails on the far side, on the one box that cannot fix it. It exists only so a box with
# no file(1) is not hard-blocked — install file(1) instead (the internet box has the internet).
# stage: you choose (optional — you own the consequences)
# BUNDLE_SKIP_STATIC_CHECK=0
# ISTIO_CHART_REPO — where `make mirror-pull` fetches the Istio charts to CARRY into the air gap (the
# air-gapped box cannot `helm repo add` anything). COMMENTED: the code default is the upstream repo, and
# an uncommented value would clobber a per-run override.
#   how: the default is https://istio-release.storage.googleapis.com/charts — override only for a mirror
# stage: you choose (optional)
# ISTIO_CHART_REPO=https://istio-release.storage.googleapis.com/charts
# acquire: choose (a toggle, not a value to obtain)
# `make mirror-verify` fast mode: check the manifest/digest only, skip full blob validation.
# MIRROR_VERIFY_FAST=0
# Gitea admin email (used when the admin account is created).
# ⚠️ COUPLED with GITEA_ADMIN_USER (~line 277) — see the note there. Both, or neither.
GITEA_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@vks.local

# GITEA_LOCAL_PORT — the local port `make creds` / the e2e port-forward Gitea on.
# stage: you choose (optional — leave UNSET and the code picks a free ephemeral port)
# COMMENTED on purpose. The code reads it as `${GITEA_LOCAL_PORT:-$(pick_port)}`
# (50-seed-gitea-repos.sh:27, 99-verify.sh:25): unset ⇒ an EPHEMERAL port, so two runs on
# one box never collide. Because load_env sources this file with `set -a`, an UNCOMMENTED
# value here is exported and CLOBBERS that fallback — pinning 3000 for everyone and
# silently destroying the parallel-safety the code comments promise. Uncomment ONLY if you
# deliberately want a fixed port.
# GITEA_LOCAL_PORT=3000
#
# APP_LOCAL_PORT — the local port `make verify` port-forwards the app on (99-verify.sh:26).
# stage: you choose (optional — leave UNSET and the code picks a free ephemeral port)
# Same rule as GITEA_LOCAL_PORT: read as `${APP_LOCAL_PORT:-$(pick_port)}`, so leave it COMMENTED
# or you pin the port for everyone and lose parallel-run safety.
# APP_LOCAL_PORT=8080

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Demo application
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO APP_NAME here: it is per-app (apps/registry.tsv). APP_BRANCH and APP_REPLICAS below ARE global
# — they apply to every app equally, so a single value is correct.
# Branch of each <app>-app repo that a push triggers CI on.
APP_BRANCH=main
# Container port the app binds — consumed by the app Dockerfile (`ARG APP_INTERNAL_PORT`) and by
# local `make app-run`. It does NOT change the in-cluster port: deploy/javawebapp/deployment.yaml pins
# containerPort 8080 (and SERVER_PORT=8080), because that manifest is seeded into the javawebapp-deploy
# repo and is not rendered from this file. Changing this alone will NOT move the pod's port.
APP_INTERNAL_PORT=8080
# Host port for LOCAL dev runs (mvn spring-boot:run / java -jar). check-ports guards it.
# BUILDER_TMPFS_SIZE — size of the tmpfs the app targets stage their work tree into.
#
# app-test, check-ui-contract, trivy-fs and app-run all run inside the app's builder image with the
# source bind-mounted READ-ONLY, and copy it into a tmpfs at /work. That is what keeps a local run
# from ever writing into your tree (under rootful docker a writable bind mount leaves ROOT-OWNED
# files behind; under rootless podman `--user` is backwards).
#
# when you need it: a build whose work tree exceeds the default — rust's target/ or dotnet's obj/
# are the likely ones. The failure looks like 'No space left on device', which reads as a HOST disk
# problem and is not one.
# how: make app-test BUILDER_TMPFS_SIZE=4g
#
# COMMENTED deliberately: it is a per-run override, and load_env sources this file with `set -a`
# AFTER make puts the override in the environment, so an uncommented value would silently win.
# The code's own ${BUILDER_TMPFS_SIZE:-2g} supplies the default. This is a RAM-backed tmpfs.
# BUILDER_TMPFS_SIZE=2g

# APP_DEV_PORT — COMMENTED DELIBERATELY. It is a PER-RUN override (`make app-run APP_DEV_PORT=8099`),
# and load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER make has put the override in the environment,
# so an uncommented value silently WINS. MEASURED 2026-08-23: `make app-run APP_DEV_PORT=8099` ran
# check-ports against 8099, then bound 8080 and died 'address already in use'. The code's own
# `${APP_DEV_PORT:-8080}` supplies the default; this line only defeated the override.
# APP_DEV_PORT=8080
# Replicas ArgoCD deploys.
APP_REPLICAS=2
# Air-gap Maven builder image tag (deps pre-baked; built by scripts/15-build-push-builder.sh).
# Its ref is derived as $HARBOR_URL/$HARBOR_INFRA_PROJECT/javawebapp-builder:$BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG.
# Rebuild + bump when apps/java/javawebapp/pom.xml dependencies change.
# BUILDER_FRESHNESS_ENFORCE — 1 makes a stale/unstamped builder image FATAL instead of a WARN.
# Default 0 (grace period): on the day the io.vks.builder.inputs stamp landed no image carried
# it, so enforcing immediately would have failed all four gates at once and demanded six full
# rebuilds. Flip to 1 once the fleet has rebuilt via `make builder-image`.
# BUILDER_FRESHNESS_ENFORCE=0

# BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG — COMMENTED DELIBERATELY. Uncommented, load_env's `set -a` sources this file
# AFTER the ambient environment, so it CLOBBERS a per-run override: MEASURED 2026-08-23,
# `BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG=9.9.9` + load_env yielded 0.3.0. Worse, `make` -include's .env/.env.state/
# .env.kind but NEVER this file, so an uncommented value here reached only the load_env
# consumers (14-builder-build, 60-configure-tekton, app-run) and not the three hermetic gates
# (app-test, trivy-fs, check-ui-contract) -- a bump split the builder from the gate certifying it.
# The default now lives ONCE, in lib/apps.sh as BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG_DEFAULT. Override per run:
#   make app-test BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG=0.4.0     (or export it)
# BUILDER_IMAGE_TAG=0.3.0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. VKS access (VCF 9 + Supervisor) — see scripts/30-vks-login.sh
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ACQUISITION ORDER (real lab): (A) set the upfront vCenter vars — SUPERVISOR_HOST,
# VKS_NAMESPACE, VKS_CLUSTER_NAME, VKS_USERNAME; (B) get the workload kubeconfig with
# `vcf cluster kubeconfig get` (see KUBECONFIG how: below); (C/D) DISCOVER Harbor's and
# ArgoCD's LB IPs AFTER you install them in Part A (see §1/§4 how: comments); (E) you
# CHOOSE the Gitea passwords (§2) for the components WE install. KinD needs NONE of this:
# the kind steps auto-DISCOVER every value and write them to a gitignored .env.kind overlay
# (sourced last), so `make e2e-kind` runs with zero manual .env setup.
#
# Everything downstream only needs a working KUBECONFIG + context. 30-vks-login.sh
# is the single pluggable step that produces them.
# stage: generated by `vcf cluster kubeconfig get` (real lab) / auto in KinD (.env.kind)
# how (INFERRED — Broadcom 9.0-via-redirect docs; we have never run it on a lab. Auto-written by KinD.):
#      vcf cluster kubeconfig get $VKS_CLUSTER_NAME --export-file $KUBECONFIG   (after `vcf context create/use`) (inferred)
#      legacy 8.x: kubectl vsphere login --tanzu-kubernetes-cluster-name $VKS_CLUSTER_NAME \
#                    --tanzu-kubernetes-cluster-namespace $VKS_NAMESPACE --server $SUPERVISOR_HOST --vsphere-username $VKS_USERNAME
#
# COMMENTED, and that is load-bearing. load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER your
# environment is established, so an UNCOMMENTED value here silently CLOBBERS a per-run override:
#     make gitops KUBECONFIG=/path/to/other.kubeconfig   -> was IGNORED, you targeted the default
# It is also DISCOVERED (the KinD flow publishes it) and set by `make vks-login`. The code default
# applies when it is unset — see scripts/lib/os.sh (load_env).
# KUBECONFIG=./secrets/vks.kubeconfig
# KUBECONFIG context to select after login (the context name INSIDE the kubeconfig).
# Distinct from VKS_CONTEXT_NAME below (the vcf-CLI context NAME, only for VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vcf).
# COMMENTED, and load-bearing. This SELECTS which kube context you talk to
# (30-vks-login.sh: `kubectl config use-context "$VKS_CONTEXT"`). Uncommented, load_env's `set -a`
# exported it OVER a per-run override, so `VKS_CONTEXT=my-lab-ctx make ...` was silently ignored —
# the same bug as ARGOCD_SERVER (#174). The default is applied in code (lib/os.sh).
# VKS_CONTEXT=vks-workload
# Auth method 30-vks-login.sh uses to produce KUBECONFIG + context (code default: kubeconfig).
#   kubeconfig = use the KUBECONFIG above as-is (KinD writes this into .env.kind automatically)
#   vcf        = vcf-CLI login (real VKS lab)   |   vsphere = kubectl-vsphere plugin login
# Commented so the code default applies and a `make vks-login VKS_AUTH_METHOD=…` override is not clobbered.
# VKS_AUTH_METHOD=kubeconfig
#
# --- VCF-CLI login inputs (VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vcf; real VKS lab) ---
# The context NAME is POSITIONAL; only the password is prompted (never on argv). Lab-verified 2026-07-22:
#   vcf context create <VKS_CONTEXT_NAME> --endpoint $SUPERVISOR_HOST \
#       [--insecure-skip-tls-verify] --auth-type basic          # BARE host — no https:// scheme
#   vcf context use <VKS_CONTEXT_NAME>:<VKS_NAMESPACE>
# stage: set upfront (vCenter)
# where: vCenter → Workload Management → Supervisors → Control Plane IP
# SUPERVISOR_HOST=<SET-IN-.env>       # Supervisor / VCF endpoint (host or IP, no scheme)
# stage: OPTIONAL — a PER-ATTEMPT cap on reading the certificate SUPERVISOR_HOST:443 presents
#                   (fetch-supervisor-ca.sh). It is NOT a total budget.
# MEASURED 2026-08-11: that handshake costs 19-24 ms on a healthy lab (3 runs, same box), so 10s
# already tolerates a ~400x slowdown. Raising it does NOT help the failure it looks like it should:
# a walk died here after exactly 10s while the SAME box completed the SAME handshake in 24 ms ten
# minutes later — the endpoint was DOWN for >=10s (a Supervisor VIP reconciling after a service
# install/uninstall), not slow. A bigger cap only helps if you can guess the outage's length, and it
# re-creates what this cap exists to prevent: a WRONG SUPERVISOR_HOST hanging in silence (132s,
# which is why the cap was added).
# how: leave it alone unless a probe is genuinely slow; per run, e.g.
#      make fetch-supervisor-ca SUPERVISOR_TLS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=20
# SUPERVISOR_TLS_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# stage: OPTIONAL — the WALL-CLOCK budget fetch-supervisor-ca.sh retries within, and the pause
#                   between attempts. The per-attempt cap above is NOT a budget; this is.
# The Supervisor's control-plane IP is a FLOATING IP bound to whichever control-plane VM holds the
# etcd leader lease, re-evaluated every 5s (Broadcom KB 313123). During leader churn — which a
# Supervisor-Service install/uninstall or a namespace delete provokes — it is bound to NO node and
# the endpoint is simply GONE for a few seconds. That is normal, so it is retried. A WRONG host is
# NOT retried: a name that does not resolve, or a port that refuses, fails in about a second.
# 600s is sized from this repo's own precedent for the same class (lib/vcenter.sh's
# VC_SS_INSTALL_RETRIES, 60 x 10s), not from taste. Patience is free here — fetch-supervisor-ca is
# not on the install-all path, so nothing automated waits on it.
# how: per run, e.g. make fetch-supervisor-ca SUPERVISOR_TLS_BUDGET_SECONDS=1200
# SUPERVISOR_TLS_BUDGET_SECONDS=600
# SUPERVISOR_TLS_RETRY_SECONDS=10
# stage: OPTIONAL — connect timeout for the reachability PROBE that classifies a failure
#                   (dns-failure / refused / timeout / half-open) so the error names what it saw.
# SUPERVISOR_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make show-dns-records` waits for the Harbor/ArgoCD LoadBalancer to be
#                   given an address, before reporting that there is nothing to create.
# a real default: 0 — one read, no waiting. scenario-1 §4 passes a budget because it runs the target
# immediately after issuing the install, when the address does not exist yet.
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: show-dns-records.sh calls load_env, so an uncommented 0 would be exported and
# would silently defeat `make show-dns-records DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_SECONDS=900`.
# how: per run, e.g. make show-dns-records DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_SECONDS=0
# stage: OPTIONAL — how often that wait re-reads the LoadBalancer.
# a real default: 15.
# how: per run, e.g. make show-dns-records DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_SECONDS=900 DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=30
# DNS_RECORDS_WAIT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=15
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make harbor-reachable` waits for Harbor to actually answer, after you
#                   have created its A record.
# a real default: 900 — it waits WITHOUT being asked, because a reader who types the bare command
# must not get the failure the wait exists to prevent. MEASURED on a real lab 2026-08-12: 7m25s from
# `install issued` to the first answer, so 900 is ~2x that. Set 0 to sample once and return.
# It costs `make lab-preflight` NOTHING: that calls the shared reporter directly, not this target.
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: 04-harbor-reachable.sh calls load_env, so an uncommented value would be
# exported and would silently defeat `make harbor-reachable HARBOR_REACHABLE_WAIT_SECONDS=0`.
# how: per run, e.g. make harbor-reachable HARBOR_REACHABLE_WAIT_SECONDS=0
# HARBOR_REACHABLE_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make argocd-address` waits for svc/argocd-server to exist AND be given
#                   a LoadBalancer address.
# a real default: 900. MEASURED on two walk rows: the ArgoCD Deployment first appears ~5-6 min after
# the install is issued (row1 14:32:29->14:37:46, row3 16:01:12->16:08:08); the LB address follows.
# Set 0 to sample once and return.
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: 09-argocd-address.sh calls load_env, so an uncommented value would be exported
# and would silently defeat `make argocd-address ARGOCD_ADDRESS_WAIT_SECONDS=0`.
# how: per run, e.g. make argocd-address ARGOCD_ADDRESS_WAIT_SECONDS=0
# ARGOCD_ADDRESS_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make vks-k8s-version` waits for a Ready+Compatible TKr to appear.
# a real default: 900. MEASURED 2026-08-12: a just-enabled Supervisor listed 8 releases with ZERO
# Ready, and the newest went Ready+Compatible about a minute later — a point-in-time read would have
# told the operator the lab had nothing usable. Set 0 to sample once.
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: same reason as above.
# how: per run, e.g. make vks-k8s-version VKS_TKR_WAIT_SECONDS=0
# VKS_TKR_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# `make argocd-password` — the ArgoCD operator creates argocd-initial-admin-secret a little AFTER
# the instance is requested. MEASURED 2026-08-12: scenario-1 Step 5 read it 12 seconds too early and
# the error told the operator to go and get the password from their lab. COMMENTED like its three
# siblings: an uncommented value here would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and silently defeat
# `make argocd-password ARGOCD_PASSWORD_WAIT_SECONDS=0`. (creds.sh does not rely on that — it passes
# `--wait 0` as an ARGUMENT, which nothing in any .env can reach.)
# how: make argocd-password ARGOCD_PASSWORD_WAIT_SECONDS=0    # fail fast instead of waiting
# ARGOCD_PASSWORD_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make creds-show` / `make argocd-password` let a single kubectl lookup
#                   run before giving up and printing <not set>.
# a real default: 10. It bounds WALL TIME, which `--request-timeout` does not: measured against a
# blackholed endpoint, `--request-timeout=3s get` still took 15.0s (kubectl retries the dial) and an
# unbounded `get secret` took 150.1s — 89% of a 168s report. 10 is deliberately generous relative to
# a healthy lookup; it is NOT measured against a live cluster, so raise it if a slow-but-healthy lab
# starts reporting <not set>.
# how: per run, e.g. make creds-show CREDS_K8S_TIMEOUT=30
# CREDS_K8S_TIMEOUT=10
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make vks-cluster-create` waits for BOTH the advertised control-plane
#                   endpoint and its LoadBalancer IP to become readable, so it can compare them and
#                   fail fast on a stale endpoint instead of burning the ~30-minute readiness wait.
# a real default: 90. Both values appeared within ~35s on a single-host 9.1 lab; 90 is headroom.
#                 Seeing NEITHER by the deadline is not a failure — the check DECLINES to judge.
# how: per run, e.g. make vks-cluster-create VKS_CP_ENDPOINT_WAIT_SECONDS=180
# VKS_CP_ENDPOINT_WAIT_SECONDS=90
# --- Supervisor platform + VKS Standard Packages (the `##@ Supervisor platform` targets) ----------
# EVERY knob in this block STAYS COMMENTED. All four scripts that read them call load_env, which
# sources this file with `set -a` AFTER make has put a per-run override into the environment — so an
# uncommented value here would silently defeat `make <target> VAR=<v>`. Same reason the
# SUPERVISOR_TLS_* knobs above are commented. This is the .env.example clobber class.
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make wcp-status|wcp-restart|wcp-start|wcp-stop` waits for vCenter's
#                   Workload Management (wcp) service to reach the state you asked for.
# a real default: 600. A wcp restart is minutes on a loaded vCenter, not seconds.
# how: per run, e.g. make wcp-restart WCP_WAIT_SECONDS=900
# WCP_WAIT_SECONDS=600

# WCP_POLL_SECONDS - how often the wcp wait loop re-probes, in WHOLE SECONDS. Default 10.
# Each probe re-logs-in (the API and its session both disappear during a restart), so this is
# also how often vCenter is asked for a session -- do not make it aggressive.
# LEAVE THIS COMMENTED: it is a per-run knob, and load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER
# the caller's environment, so an uncommented value would BEAT `make wcp-restart WCP_POLL_SECONDS=..`.
# how: per run, e.g. make wcp-restart WCP_POLL_SECONDS=30
# WCP_POLL_SECONDS=10
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make uninstall-supervisor-service` waits for the service to be gone.
# a real default: 1800 — deliberately longer than the others: an uninstall drains every tenant's
# workloads before the service namespace disappears.
# how: per run, e.g. make uninstall-supervisor-service SERVICE=<id> CONFIRM=yes UNINSTALL_SERVICE_WAIT_SECONDS=2400
# UNINSTALL_SERVICE_WAIT_SECONDS=1800
# stage: OPTIONAL — the namespace `make list-vks-packages|install-vks-package|uninstall-vks-package`
#                   reads Package / PackageInstall objects from, on the GUEST cluster.
# a real default: vmware-system-tkg, where VKS publishes its Standard Packages.
# how: per run, e.g. make list-vks-packages VKS_PACKAGE_NAMESPACE=<ns>
# VKS_PACKAGE_NAMESPACE=vmware-system-tkg
# stage: OPTIONAL — how long `make install-vks-package` waits for the PackageInstall to reconcile.
# a real default: 600.
# how: per run, e.g. make install-vks-package PACKAGE=<name> VKS_PACKAGE_WAIT_SECONDS=1200
# VKS_PACKAGE_WAIT_SECONDS=600
# stage: OPTIONAL — a values file for `make install-vks-package` (becomes a <pkg>-pkg-values Secret).
#                   Unset means the package's own defaults.
# how: write the package's values YAML to a file and point at it, per run:
#      make install-vks-package PACKAGE=<name> PKG_VALUES=./secrets/<name>-values.yaml
# PKG_VALUES=
# stage: OPTIONAL — pin the version `make install-vks-package` installs.
# a real default: the newest version the CLUSTER offers — vks-package.sh reads
# `${PKG_VERSION:-$(… | tail -1)}`, i.e. it DISCOVERS it. An uncommented value defeats that.
# how: `make list-vks-packages` prints what the cluster offers; then per run:
#      make install-vks-package PACKAGE=<name> PKG_VERSION=<v>
# PKG_VERSION=
# stage: OPTIONAL — the FQDN `make show-dns-records` prints for the ArgoCD A record, when you front
#                   ArgoCD with a name instead of reaching its LoadBalancer IP directly.
# a real default: ARGOCD_SERVER — show-dns-records.sh reads `${ARGOCD_HOST:-${ARGOCD_SERVER:-…}}`,
# so unset it falls back to the address you already set. An uncommented value defeats that fallback.
# how: you choose it — the name your REAL DNS will answer for ArgoCD, e.g. argocd.example.com
# ARGOCD_HOST=
# stage: OPTIONAL on the vcf path (auto-discovered when unset) — REQUIRED for VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vsphere
# where: the vSphere Namespace name in vCenter (the <ns> in `context use <name>:<ns>`)
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: 30-vks-login.sh DISCOVERS this from `vcf context list` after the context is
# created (lib/os.sh vks_discover_namespace). load_env sources this file with `set -a`, so an
# uncommented value would be exported and would silently defeat that discovery — the clobber class
# `make check-env-clobber` gates. Set it only to PIN one when discovery reports several candidates.
# VKS_NAMESPACE=<SET-IN-.env>         # the vSphere SUPERVISOR namespace where the guest cluster is provisioned
#                                     #   and where the Supervisor Services (Harbor, ArgoCD) run — NOT a k8s
#                                     #   namespace inside the guest cluster. (The <ns> in `vcf context use <name>:<ns>`.)
# stage: OPTIONAL (defaults, announced at login) — REQUIRED for VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vsphere
# MUST STAY COMMENTED: the code applies the default, so an uncommented value here would clobber a
# per-run `make vks-login VKS_USERNAME=…` override.
# ⚠️ THE SSO DOMAIN IS A PARAMETER, NOT A CONSTANT, and the built-in default is only one lab's answer.
#    MEASURED on two real 9.1 labs: `vsphere.local` on one, `wld.sso` on the other. Neither is "the"
#    value — that is exactly why vks_username() WARNS every time it applies the default instead of
#    accepting it silently: a plausible-but-wrong principal takes a real password at an interactive
#    prompt and then fails somewhere else, or succeeds as the wrong user.
# where: vCenter → Administration → Single Sign On → Users and Groups → the *Domain* dropdown. It is
#        the domain you already log into vCenter with; there is no CLI for it that we can run here.
# VKS_USERNAME=<SET-IN-.env>          # 'user@SSO.DOMAIN' (e.g. administrator@vsphere.local). Set it whenever
#                                     #   your lab is not the default SSO domain, or you log in as a tenant user.
# VKS_SSO_DOMAIN=<SET-IN-.env>        # PREFER THIS when only the domain differs: it is appended to a bare
#                                     #   VKS_USERNAME, and it is honoured on the DEFAULT path too (e.g.
#                                     #   vsphere.local — measured on this lab; WLD.SSO on the other).
# VKS_CONTEXT_NAME=<SET-IN-.env>      # the vcf-CLI context NAME, passed POSITIONALLY (e.g. sup66)
#                                     #   — DISTINCT from VKS_CONTEXT (kubeconfig context) above
# Commented so the code default (false) applies and a `make vks-login VKS_INSECURE_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY=1`
# / `.env` override is not clobbered. Set true to skip verifying the Supervisor's self-signed cert.
# VKS_INSECURE_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY=true
#
# --- kubectl-vsphere plugin login inputs (VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vsphere; pre-9 Supervisor) ---
# Reuses SUPERVISOR_HOST / VKS_NAMESPACE / VKS_USERNAME / VKS_INSECURE_SKIP_TLS_VERIFY above, plus:
# stage: set upfront (vCenter) — the VKS cluster name in vCenter (also feeds `vcf cluster kubeconfig get`)
# VKS_CLUSTER_NAME=<SET-IN-.env>      # the GUEST/workload Kubernetes cluster (a "VKS cluster") provisioned inside
#                                     #   VKS_NAMESPACE — DISTINCT from the Supervisor. This is where Gitea / Tekton /
#                                     #   javawebapp / Istio deploy; `vcf cluster kubeconfig get $VKS_CLUSTER_NAME` exports
#                                     #   ITS kubeconfig to $KUBECONFIG. (NB: the VKS-provided ArgoCD runs on the
#                                     #   SUPERVISOR, not here — see ARGOCD_NAMESPACE §4 for the deploy-target topology.)
#
# ---- guest-cluster SHAPE (scenario-1 §4b) — DISCOVER on your Supervisor, then pin ----------
# NONE of these is a constant. They are read off YOUR Supervisor; the values shown are what ONE
# 9.1 lab reported on 2026-08-04 and are examples, never defaults to copy.
# All COMMENTED: each is a per-run override with a code-side fallback, so an uncommented value
# here would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and silently defeat it (check-env-clobber gates it).
#
# stage: discover (after the Supervisor is up, before creating the guest cluster)
# how: kubectl get clusterclass -n vmware-system-vks-public
# ⚠️ NOT a constant — one lab carried SIX (builtin-generic-v3.1.0 … v3.6.0), all READY.
# VKS_CLUSTERCLASS=builtin-generic-v3.6.0
#
# stage: discover
# how: kubectl get kubernetesreleases
# ⚠️ Must be BOTH Ready=True AND Compatible=True, and it comes from the Supervisor-level content
#    library — a lab with a different subscription has a different set. Anything else is rejected
#    at admission, with a message about the version rather than about this variable.
# VKS_K8S_VERSION=v1.32.10+vmware.1-fips-vkr.2
#
# stage: discover  (leave it alone unless your ClusterClass selects a non-photon OS)
# how: kubectl get osimages -o custom-columns=K8S:.spec.kubernetesVersion,OS:.spec.os.name
# ⚠️ This is NOT ours — `os-name=photon` is the builtin-generic ClusterClass default, and the
#    admission webhook resolves a KubernetesRelease AND an OSImage for that OS. `make
#    vks-k8s-version` only offers releases that HAVE an image for this OS, because the two arrive on
#    independent timelines: MEASURED 2026-08-16, a release was Ready+Compatible for 8 minutes before
#    its photon image existed, and a create in that window was rejected with "Could not resolve
#    KR/OSImage" naming a release that `kubectl get kubernetesreleases` showed as True True.
#    Change this only if your ClusterClass's osImageSelector asks for a different OS.
# VKS_OS_NAME=photon
#
# stage: discover
# how: kubectl get virtualmachineclass
# ⚠️ `best-effort-*` classes carry NO reservation, so a namespace resource-pool limit (if your
#    platform's template sets one) becomes the real ceiling. See §4a on sizing.
# VKS_VM_CLASS=best-effort-small
#
# stage: choose
# how: leave the default; raise it only if `kubectl top pod -n istio-system` shows istiod RSS
#      approaching it after `make verify` (see scripts/46-install-istio.sh for the measurement).
# ISTIOD_MEMORY_REQUEST is istiod's memory RESERVATION for the Istio we install
# (INGRESS_CONTROLLER=istio). The chart default 2048Mi does NOT FIT the sizing this file documents:
# MEASURED 2026-08-24, best-effort-small workers leave at most 1918Mi free even perfectly balanced
# -- 130Mi short, with NO placement that works, so istiod sat Pending 42m and the ingress install
# timed out. Not a flake: the demo GREW -- the runs that installed istio at 2048Mi carried TWO
# apps and it now carries SIX, so a passing install was never evidence the next one would fit.
ISTIOD_MEMORY_REQUEST=768Mi
#
# stage: choose
# how: leave both unset. They tune the fit check that runs before each Istio helm release
#      (scripts/lib/capacity.sh): it refuses a pod the scheduler cannot place, turning helm's
#      300-second "Available: 0/1" timeout into a ~2-second refusal that names the shortfall.
# CAPACITY_PREFLIGHT=0 disables that check entirely. Set it only to get past a check you believe
# is wrong -- it does not make the pod schedulable, it just restores the illegible timeout.
# CAPACITY_PREFLIGHT=1
#
# CAPACITY_WARN_MARGIN_MI is how much headroom (Mi) must remain after the pod fits before the
# check stops warning. Below it the install still proceeds, but it prints the per-node table so a
# shrinking margin is visible BEFORE it becomes a failure -- the 2026-08-21 config cleared by
# ~77Mi and nobody could have known.
# CAPACITY_WARN_MARGIN_MI=256
#
# stage: discover
# how: kubectl get storageclass
# ⚠️ Guest node ROOT DISKS appear as PVCs against this class — MEASURED ~20 GiB EACH, so a 2-node
#    cluster costs ~40 GiB here before any application volume.
# ⚠️ NOT A CONSTANT, and the two real 9.1 labs behind these docs disagree — the name follows the
#    STORAGE BACKING, so copying either one is a coin flip:
#      wcp-vmfs                      single-host VMFS  (this lab)
#      vsan-default-storage-policy   vSAN             (the other lab)
#    A Supervisor storage class is the storage policy assigned to the namespace, with its name
#    NORMALIZED — the vSphere Client shows the policy "vSAN Default Storage Policy", the class is
#    `vsan-default-storage-policy` (lowercased, spaces -> '-'). Use the vSphere Client when you do
#    not have a Supervisor kubeconfig yet (scenario-1 §2 needs this value BEFORE §3 authenticates),
#    but normalize it and re-check with `kubectl get storageclass` once you do: the UI string pasted
#    verbatim is not a valid Kubernetes object name, so no PVC ever binds. The same value is what
#    Harbor's data-values file wants in place of `insert-storage-class-name-here`.
# VKS_STORAGE_CLASS=wcp-vmfs
#
# stage: set upfront — the guest cluster's shape
# how: you choose; 1 control-plane node is the lab default, 3 for anything resembling production
# VKS_CONTROL_PLANE_COUNT=1
#
# stage: set upfront
# how: you choose; each worker costs a VM class + a root-disk PVC (see VKS_STORAGE_CLASS)
# ⚠️ NOT YET READ BY ANY SCRIPT (measured 2026-08-05: 0 readers, as for VKS_CLUSTERCLASS,
#    VKS_K8S_VERSION, VKS_VM_CLASS, VKS_STORAGE_CLASS and VKS_CONTROL_PLANE_COUNT). It records
#    intent and is the input the §4a/§4b automation will consume; today the cluster is created by
#    the manifest you apply, so set the count THERE. Setting it here alone changes nothing.
# 🔴 AND THE DEFAULT BELOW IS TOO SMALL. MEASURED on a real 9.1 lab: ONE best-effort-small worker
#    is 10m SHORT of the platform — allocatable 1930m, platform requests 1840m (95%),
#    tekton-pipelines-webhook wants 100m -> FailedScheduling. The control plane does not help (no
#    toleration on our pods), and 95% was before any pipeline ran. Use 2. See scenario-1 §4b.
# VKS_NODE_COUNT=1
#
# --- node ephemeral storage (GiB) --------------------------------------------------------------
# 🔴 THE STOCK NODE DISK IS 20 GiB AND THAT IS TOO SMALL FOR SIX BUILDERS. VKS node images ship a
#    STATIC 20 GiB root partition (Broadcom KB 408602, whose listed symptoms are literally "Nodes
#    report DiskPressure" and "Pods are evicted due to insufficient ephemeral storage"). MEASURED
#    on this lab: 19.3 GiB capacity per node. Our six builder images alone are 4.44 GiB — rust
#    1.57, go 1.00, dotnet 0.97, java 0.62, nodejs 0.18, python 0.11 — before Kaniko, six app
#    runtimes, Tekton, Istio, and six builds' writable layers.
#
#    WHAT IT COSTS. The kubelet taints the node NoSchedule, Tekton's affinity assistant has already
#    pinned the PipelineRun to it, and the next task cannot move ("Preemption is not helpful for
#    scheduling"). REPRODUCED in a throwaway 3-node KinD cluster 2026-08-25: Tekton's own recovery
#    never fired across three arms (plain taint / cordon / forced reconcile); removing the taint
#    scheduled the pod in under 5s. So the stall lasts exactly as long as the taint does.
#    HOW LONG that is is NOT ESTABLISHED: the stalls cluster tightly (~297s ± 22s) but at least
#    THREE 5-minute constants sit in this path, and an adversary round showed the tightness fits
#    the scheduler's unschedulable-queue flush BETTER than the kubelet's eviction damper. Either
#    way it is minutes. See k8s/vks/cluster.yaml for the full note and what would settle it.
#
#    50 is sized from that footprint plus real GC headroom (steady state well under the 85%
#    image-GC trigger), NOT copied from a cloud vendor's default. Leave both COMMENTED — the code
#    defaults them, and an uncommented value here would be exported by load_env and clobber a
#    per-run `make vks-cluster-create VKS_NODE_CONTAINERD_GIB=…` override.
# VKS_NODE_CONTAINERD_GIB=50
# VKS_NODE_KUBELET_GIB=50
#
# ⚠️ THE THREE ABOVE (VKS_CLUSTERCLASS / VKS_CONTROL_PLANE_COUNT / VKS_NODE_COUNT) ARE NOW READ —
# `make vks-cluster-create` is their reader. Leave them COMMENTED and the script defaults them in
# CODE: builtin-generic-v3.6.0 / 1 / 2. Note the code default for VKS_NODE_COUNT is **2**, not the
# 1 shown above, because the measured note above says one worker is 10m short of the platform.
#
# stage: optional (make vks-cluster-create) — the namespace the ClusterClass lives in
# how: kubectl get clusterclass -A   (measured on a 9.1 lab: vmware-system-vks-public)
# VKS_CLUSTERCLASS_NAMESPACE=vmware-system-vks-public
#
# stage: optional (make vks-cluster-create) — the guest cluster's INTERNAL pod/service ranges.
# They are per-cluster and do not need to be unique across clusters on the same Supervisor.
# how: leave unset unless your platform team assigns ranges; these mirror the lab default.
# VKS_POD_CIDR=172.20.0.0/16
# VKS_SERVICE_CIDR=172.21.0.0/16
#
# stage: optional (make vks-cluster-status) — how long to WAIT for readiness, and how often to poll.
# ⚠️ COMMENTED ON PURPOSE. VKS_CLUSTER_POLL_SECONDS was BORN because POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS used to
# be uncommented, so load_env's `set -a` re-exported it and CLOBBERED a per-run override (measured
# at the time: `POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=20 make vks-cluster-status` silently polled every 5s). That
# is HISTORY as of B122 — POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS is now commented and no longer clobbers anything.
# (The old wording also said "above"; it is ~190 lines BELOW, in section 8. That was wrong before
# B122 too.)
# The key STAYS, on a reason decided now rather than inherited: this loop makes FOUR kubectl calls
# per iteration (report() 1 + nodes_ready() 3) against an 1800s budget, where every
# POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS site makes one against 300s. 15s is the right cadence for this loop and 5s
# is not — a different value earns its own key, which is the same rule the rest of section 8 follows.
# scripts/test-cluster-status-wait-gate.sh:68 also consumes it.
# how: per run, e.g. make vks-cluster-status VKS_CLUSTER_WAIT_SECONDS=1800
# VKS_CLUSTER_WAIT_SECONDS=0
# VKS_CLUSTER_POLL_SECONDS=15
#
#
# stage: optional (make uninstall-all) — bounds on the two waits that can hang forever.
# An ArgoCD Application whose destination cluster is already gone can never finish its finalizer,
# and a CAPI Cluster is held by two controllers; uninstall-all refuses to strip either automatically.
# how: leave unset unless a teardown is legitimately slow on your lab.
# UNINSTALL_APP_WAIT_SECONDS=120
# UNINSTALL_CLUSTER_WAIT_SECONDS=900
#
# stage: per-run ONLY (make uninstall-all) — the typed confirmation. NEVER set this in .env: it exists so
# a destructive teardown cannot run without the operator naming the cluster on the command line.
# how: make uninstall-all CONFIRM=<your VKS_CLUSTER_NAME>
# CONFIRM=
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# YOUR SSO PASSWORD — SET IT IN .env, like every other value here.
#
# Two names take it, and on a standard lab they are the SAME value. Both belong in .env:
#   VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD   the vcf CLI's login to the SUPERVISOR (VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vcf)
#   VCENTER_PASSWORD           the vCenter REST session that creates the vSphere Namespace
#                              and installs Harbor / ArgoCD as Supervisor Services
#
# .env is gitignored, and every consumer reads these from the environment or from a
# `umask 077` curl -K config file — never from a command line, so they never reach `ps`.
#
# SINGLE-QUOTE THESE TWO VALUES:   VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD='S0me:Pass$word'
#   `load_env` sources this file with `set -a`, so the SHELL PARSES the value. Measured, seven
#   realistic passwords written bare: `VMware1!` and `Str0ng#Pass` survive; `a b c`, `quo'te` and
#   `dou"ble` fail to parse; `back\slash` loses the backslash; and `P@ssw0rd$1` becomes
#   `P@ssw0rd<the path of the file being sourced>` — because `$1` is the SOURCING SHELL'S FIRST
#   POSITIONAL PARAMETER, not an empty string. A backtick is worse still: inside a double-quoted
#   or bare value it is COMMAND SUBSTITUTION. Single-quoted, all seven survive; escape an embedded
#   `'` as `'\''`.
#
#   THIS IS NOT A RULE ABOUT `.env` — it is a rule about THESE PASSWORD KEYS. `.env` really does
#   have two parsers, and make keeps quotes LITERALLY (measured: `P='VMware1!'` -> make sees
#   `'VMware1!'`). Values make DOES expand and pass as argv — HARBOR_URL, HARBOR_CA_FILE,
#   ARGOCD_SERVER — must stay bare, or you ship `'./secrets/harbor-ca.crt'` as an argument. The
#   passwords are safe to quote only because NOTHING reads them make-side; verify that still holds:
#     grep -nE '\$[({](VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD|VCENTER_PASSWORD)[)}]' Makefile   # want: no hits
#
#   ⚠️ DO NOT WRITE `$$`. An earlier version of this comment said to double a literal `$` as `$$`
#   because make expands it. In the SHELL — the only consumer here — `$$` is THE PROCESS ID, so
#   `P@ssw0rd$$1` yields A DIFFERENT PASSWORD ON EVERY INVOCATION. Against an SSO account that
#   locks out after 3 failures with no token refresh, that spends the budget in three runs and is
#   undiagnosable by reading the file.
#
#   The same rule the CODE already uses (`set_env_var` in scripts/lib/os.sh): quote when the value
#   contains anything outside `A-Za-z0-9_.:/@+=-`. A `*` is fine bare, contrary to what this
#   comment used to claim.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# stage: real lab, Step 1 — you supply this
# VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vCenter REST API — used to CREATE the vSphere Namespace and INSTALL Harbor /
# ArgoCD as Supervisor Services (scenario-1 Steps 1b, 2, 3) without a browser.
#
# These calls get a vCenter-wide session (create namespaces, install/uninstall Supervisor
# Services), whereas the vcf CLI authenticates to the SUPERVISOR. On a standard lab it is
# the same identity and the same password as VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD — set both in .env.
# The password reaches curl through a `umask 077` -K config file, never argv.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# stage: real lab, before Step 1b — you supply these
# how: VCENTER_HOST is the vCenter FQDN you collected in Step 0 (NOT the Supervisor IP)
# VCENTER_HOST=vcsa.env1.lab.test
# VCENTER_USERNAME=administrator@vsphere.local
# VCENTER_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>          # same value as VCF_CLI_VSPHERE_PASSWORD on a standard lab
#
# ── make vks-trust-probe ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read-only probe: does this guest cluster trust our Harbor, and by what mechanism? It creates a
# THROWAWAY namespace, runs one PSA-restricted-compliant pod with imagePullPolicy: Always to force a
# real registry round-trip, and deletes the namespace on exit.
#
# All COMMENTED: these are per-run knobs, and an uncommented value here would be exported by
# load_env's `set -a` and clobber a `make vks-trust-probe PROBE_NS=...` override.
#
# PROBE_NS               throwaway namespace name          (default: vks-trust-probe-$$)
# PROBE_IMAGE            image to pull                     (default: DERIVED from a running workload
#                                                           that has a harbor-pull secret - an
#                                                           enumerated tag would rot on the next
#                                                           pipeline run)
# PROBE_WAIT_ITERATIONS  how many polls to wait for the pull (default: 12)
# PROBE_WAIT_INTERVAL    seconds between polls               (default: 5)
# TRUST_PROBE_LOCATIONS  newline-separated <ns>/<configmap>/<key> locations to compare the CA
#                        fingerprint against (default: tkg-system/kapp-controller-config/caCerts -
#                        the consumer VKS is known to wire; the list is NOT exhaustive and the
#                        probe's output says so)
# PROBE_NS=
# PROBE_IMAGE=
# PROBE_WAIT_ITERATIONS=
# PROBE_WAIT_INTERVAL=
# TRUST_PROBE_LOCATIONS=
#
# VCENTER_CA_SHA256 - the OUT-OF-BAND digest that authenticates the vCenter anchor.
#
# COMMENTED, like every other pin here (HARBOR_CA_SHA256:176, ARGOCD_CA_SHA256:460,
# VKS_CA_SHA256:608). A pin is operator-supplied and read as ${VCENTER_CA_SHA256:-}; an UNCOMMENTED
# value would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and clobber a per-run
# `make fetch-vcenter-ca VCENTER_CA_SHA256=<digest>` override - this file's own clobber rule.
#
# Until 2026-08-21 this variable DID NOT EXIST, and the vCenter anchor was consequently checked
# NOWHERE IN ITS ENTIRE LIFE - unlike VKS_CA_SHA256, whose anchor is verified at use time by
# 30-vks-login.sh. `make fetch-vcenter-ca` now refuses to install an anchor that does not match this
# digest when you set it; with it unset the behaviour is unchanged (trust-on-first-use, and the
# CONFIRM-out-of-band warning is still the only thing standing between you and an interceptor).
#
# Get it from whoever operates vCenter, over a channel that is NOT the connection you are securing:
#   openssl x509 -in <their-ca.pem> -noout -fingerprint -sha256
# VCENTER_CA_SHA256=
#

# VCENTER_CA_FILE — the anchor that verifies vCenter's certificate. Until 2026-08-18 these calls
# used a hardcoded `-k`: the SSO ADMINISTRATOR password was sent to a peer whose identity had
# never been checked, and vCenter was the only credential-bearing TLS client in this repo with no
# trust ladder at all (Harbor, ArgoCD and the Supervisor each had one). It now FAILS CLOSED —
# the owner decision recorded in B64: vCenter is pre-existing customer infrastructure.
# COMMENTED because there is no universal default, and snapshot-protected in load_env either way,
# so `make <target> VCENTER_CA_FILE=/other.pem` wins over anything this file or .env.state says.
# ⚠️ ./secrets/supervisor-ca.crt is NOT a vCenter anchor — measured on a live lab, --cacert with
# it returns rc=60 against vCenter while the VMCA root returns rc=0. They are different CAs.
# When unset, the code looks at ./secrets/vcenter-ca.pem and refuses if that is absent too.
# how: make fetch-vcenter-ca
#      It downloads vCenter's own roots from https://<VCENTER_HOST>/certs/download.zip (the
#      "Download trusted root CA certificates" link on the vCenter landing page), picks the one
#      that actually VERIFIES vCenter — by HANDSHAKE, never by subject compare, because every lab
#      cut mints a NEW VMCA with a BYTE-IDENTICAL subject so a stale file from an earlier cut reads
#      EQUAL while verifying nothing — and writes ./secrets/vcenter-ca.pem, which is exactly where
#      this variable defaults to. It then prints the SHA-256: CONFIRM that fingerprint over a
#      channel that is NOT this connection, because the anchor was fetched from the very host it
#      is meant to authenticate.
# stage: real lab, before Step 1b (only if you use the vCenter REST path)
# VCENTER_CA_FILE=./secrets/vcenter-ca.pem
#
# VCENTER_INSECURE — skip vCenter TLS verification for THIS run. A per-run flag, not a value to
# obtain. COMMENTED, and shipped at the code default 0: an uncommented value here is exported by
# load_env's `set -a` and would silently ARM it for every run on this box, and the same file's
# other eight toggles all ship their code default so uncommenting is a no-op. Set it on the
# command line when you mean it: `VCENTER_INSECURE=1 make <target>`.
# ⚠️ It sends the SSO administrator password to an unverified peer. Never on a real lab.
# stage: you choose (a per-run flag; you own the consequences)
# VCENTER_INSECURE=0
#
# stage: real lab, Step 1b (make vsphere-namespace) — you supply these
# how: VKS_STORAGE_POLICY is the vCenter storage-policy NAME (vCenter → Policies and Profiles).
#      VKS_CLUSTER_COMPUTE is only needed when vCenter has MORE THAN ONE cluster.
# VKS_STORAGE_POLICY=<SET-IN-.env>
# VKS_VM_CLASSES=best-effort-small best-effort-medium
# VKS_CLUSTER_COMPUTE=
#
# stage: real lab, Step 2 (make install-harbor-service) — you supply this
# how: kubectl get storageclass   (against the Supervisor), or vCenter → your Namespace → Storage
# HARBOR_STORAGE_CLASS=<SET-IN-.env>
#
# stage: real lab, Step 3 (make install-argocd-service) — optional overrides
# how: leave unset; the installer asks the operator what it supports
#      (kubectl explain argocd.spec.version) and defaults the instance name to argocd-1
# ARGOCD_INSTANCE_VERSION=
# ARGOCD_INSTANCE_NAME=argocd-1
#
# stage: real lab — timeouts for the vCenter REST calls and the installs they issue
# how: leave unset. Raise VC_SS_INSTALL_RETRIES on a slow Supervisor. Installing a Supervisor
#      Service is ASYNC platform work, so the installer treats a 5xx as RETRYABLE by default and
#      fails fast on 4xx. MEASURED 2026-08-10, three distinct transient 500s in one afternoon:
#      "the service account is not ready", "signature verification result not found" (verification
#      follows registration asynchronously) and "the namespace ... is in terminating status" (a
#      reinstall racing the previous uninstall). RETRIES x INTERVAL is the budget: 60 x 10s = 10 min.
#      ARGOCD_CR_APPLY_* is the same shape one layer down: the ArgoCD operator publishes its CRD
#      BEFORE its validating webhook serves, so the instance CR apply retries on
#      "failed calling webhook" rather than treating the CRD as proof of readiness.
# VC_CONNECT_TIMEOUT=10
# VC_API_TIMEOUT=120
# VC_SS_INSTALL_RETRIES=30
# VC_SS_INSTALL_INTERVAL=10
# VKS_NAMESPACE_WAIT_SECONDS=300
# ARGOCD_CRD_WAIT_SECONDS=600
# How long 'make harbor-reachable' (and lab-preflight) waits for Harbor to answer before
# calling the address dead. Kept short on purpose: the question is 'is ANYTHING serving',
# and a live Harbor answers immediately, so a long timeout only delays a true negative.
# HARBOR_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# How long to wait for the ArgoCD Carvel PACKAGE after the CRD appears. They are published by
# the same operator but NOT at the same instant: MEASURED 2026-08-11, the CRD landed at
# 20:36:02 and the Package at 20:36:11, and a single-shot query at 20:36:10 failed the install
# on a lab where nothing was wrong.
# ARGOCD_PACKAGE_WAIT_SECONDS=180
# ARGOCD_CR_APPLY_RETRIES=30
# ARGOCD_CR_APPLY_INTERVAL=10
# VKS_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>          # vsphere method only — read via env by the plugin, never argv
#
# stage: optional (VKS_AUTH_METHOD=vcf only) — where the vcf CLI writes ITS contexts
# how: leave unset; 30-vks-login.sh defaults it to <repo>/secrets/supervisor.kubeconfig
# ⚠️ COMMENTED because it is a SELECTOR (it names WHICH cluster) — load_env snapshots it so a
#    per-run override survives, and check-env-clobber gates that it stays commented.
# ⚠️ It EXISTS because `vcf context create` writes its contexts into $KUBECONFIG and repoints
#    that file's current-context at the SUPERVISOR (MEASURED). Left to do that to a GUEST
#    kubeconfig, every later kubectl — and `make platform`/`make gitops` — silently targets
#    vSphere's control plane. Absolute path on purpose: the value is persisted into
#    ~/.config/vcf/config.yaml and resolved at USE time, so a relative one breaks from another cwd.
# VKS_SUPERVISOR_KUBECONFIG=./secrets/supervisor.kubeconfig
#
# --- Lab CLIs (`make install-vcf-clis`) — Broadcom-LICENSED, operator-supplied ---
# The VCF/VKS lab CLIs (argocd-vcf + vcf Consumption CLI + its plugin bundle). NOT needed for
# the local KinD flow (that uses the upstream argocd from deps-prereqs).
# Supply them as a FOLDER: download the artifacts (however you have entitlement — the Broadcom
# support portal, an internal mirror) on an internet box, drop them ALL in one directory, and
# set VCF_CLI_SRC_DIR to it. The folder may hold per-arch files and/or the portal's multi-arch
# "…-Binaries-…" bundles; the installer picks the right one for the jump box's OS/arch.
# The pinned versions below select the matching archive; keep them in sync with your artifacts.
# 9.1 is entitled (portal/Supervisor) — the public artifactory only serves ≤9.0.x. On an arm64 box
# argocd-vcf is amd64-only: use the upstream argocd from `make deps` and run install-vcf-cli +
# install-vcf-plugins (not all). Full guide: docs/vks-authentication.md#acquiring-the-licensed-vcf-cli-archives
ARGOCD_VCF_VERSION=v3.0.19-vcf
VCF_CLI_VERSION=9.1.0.0400.25509669
VCF_PLUGINS_VERSION=9.1.0.0400.25509793
# acquire: YOU provide it — download the licensed VCF/VKS CLI artifacts from the Broadcom portal with
#          your own entitlement and point this at the folder. They are NEVER fetched by this repo
#          (licensed binaries must not be re-hosted). Real-lab jump box only.
# VCF_CLI_SRC_DIR=<dir with the downloaded artifacts>   # REQUIRED for `make install-vcf-clis`
# BIN_DIR=~/.local/bin                                  # sudo-free install dir (default)

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Air-gap bundle (sneakernet)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUNDLE_DIR=./bundle
# Local dir crane pulls OCI layouts into before bundling / pushing.
IMAGE_CACHE_DIR=./bundle/images
# Mirror only ONE CPU architecture by default (matches the VKS/kind nodes) — keeps the
# cache ~5x smaller and mirroring much faster. Digest-pinned images (Tekton) are always
# mirrored multi-arch to preserve their list digest.
# All of the following are per-run toggles — commented so the CODE defaults apply and a
# `make … VAR=…` / env override is NOT clobbered when scripts source this file (load_env
# re-sources .env.example, so an UNcommented value here would overwrite `make mirror VAR=…`).
# MIRROR_ARCH=amd64         # single CPU arch to mirror (code default: amd64)
# MIRROR_ALL_ARCH=0         # 1 = mirror ALL architectures (larger, slower)
# MIRROR_RETRIES=5          # per-image crane retries (transient upstream/CDN resets)
# MIRROR_FORCE_PULL=0       # 1 = ignore the cache-skip sentinels, re-pull everything
# MIRROR_NO_PRUNE=0         # 1 = keep orphaned old-digest cache dirs (skip auto-prune)
# Bare-OS matrix for `make bootstrap-test` (each runs a full `make deps`, ~8-12 min/OS):
# BOOTSTRAP_TEST_OSES="ubuntu:22.04 ubuntu:24.04 ubuntu:26.04 photon:5.0"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Tunable timings (readiness polling, HTTP checks) — seconds unless noted
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# how: make install-ingress READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=900   # a slow lab; or set it in .env
# COMMENTED ON PURPOSE (B93). Every site reads it as `${READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-300}`, so leaving a
# value here made load_env's `set -a` export it and BEAT the fallback — measured, a caller's 600
# arrived as 300, i.e. the knob was inert from the command line. Commented, it is unset by default,
# the 13 code-side defaults (all 300 — verify with a LEFT word boundary, or you match the
# VERIFY_/DOCKER_ROOTLESS_/EL_ keys below and think they disagree) apply unchanged, and an
# operator's override survives.
#
# Every key in this section carries its OWN acquisition marker (the how-line above it). The honest
# status of that convention: NO GATE ENFORCES IT. Measured (B122's implementation round) — delete
# any one of those lines and BOTH check-env-coverage and check-how-provenance still exit 0, for an
# exempt key and a non-exempt one alike. So rc=0 is exactly what you get with NO marker at all, and
# an earlier draft of this very paragraph cited that rc=0 as evidence the markers were working.
# That is the vacuous green this file exists to refuse, so it is retracted here, not deleted.
# (This paragraph deliberately does NOT spell the marker prefix out: check-how-provenance greps for
# it, so a sentence ABOUT the convention gets counted AS one and inflates the gate's own
# denominator. It did — 87 counted where 85 were real — which is the same documenting-a-trap-makes-
# it-ungreppable shape the rules corpus records for hooks.)
# What the gate DOES enforce is the commented key LINE: remove `# TOOL_VERSION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5`
# and check-env-coverage goes rc=1 (measured).
# The reason to keep one marker per key is still real, just unenforced: check-env-coverage walks UP
# over a CONTIGUOUS comment block and a commented key is itself a comment line, so a run of keys
# under one shared marker would let each pass on its NEIGHBOUR's. Do not consolidate them — and if
# you want that to be more than a convention, earn it with a RED first.
# READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300
# how: make verify POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=15
# POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=5
# how: make verify-ingress CURL_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=30
# CURL_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=10
# Three sites need LONGER than the shared defaults above, and used to ask for it with a bigger
# literal fallback — which never applied, because an UNCOMMENTED value here is exported by
# load_env's `set -a` and an exported value beats any `${VAR:-...}`. So the vCenter CA fetch asked
# for 30s and silently ran with 10. Each outlier has its OWN key, because that is the only way the
# intended number is reachable at all — and each is now COMMENTED (B122), because while it was
# uncommented the key was reachable from the CODE and still inert from the COMMAND LINE. The
# numbers below are the code-side defaults, restated here so the reader can see them.
# how: make fetch-supervisor-ca VCENTER_CA_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60
# VCENTER_CA_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# how: make check-gwapi-istio-alignment GWAPI_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# GWAPI_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
# how: make uninstall-all UNINSTALL_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=20
# UNINSTALL_POLL_INTERVAL_SECONDS=10
# Same shape for READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS: 13 sites want 300, one wants 90 and one wants 600, and both
# were silently getting 300. The 600 is `make verify` waiting for a PipelineRun, so the shared
# default gave it HALF the time it asks for — a false timeout on a slow lab.
#
# ⚠️ VERIFY_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS is the SHARPEST key in this section. 99-verify.sh:23 rebinds
# `READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS="${VERIFY_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS:-600}"`, so fixing READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS
# alone (B93) left `make verify` pinned at 600 no matter what an operator asked for. Commenting
# THIS key is what actually makes `make verify` lengthenable.
# how: make verify VERIFY_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=1200
# VERIFY_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=600
# how: make engine-trust-check-rootless DOCKER_ROOTLESS_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180
# DOCKER_ROOTLESS_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=90
# make verify (99-verify.sh) — EventListener readiness + PipelineRun-trigger waits. The
# Tekton Triggers clusterInterceptor CaBundle populates AFTER the EL starts, so the EL pod
# crash-loops briefly on a fresh cluster; verify waits for it to be Ready before pushing
# (else the one-shot Gitea webhook is lost -> no PipelineRun) and re-fires once per window.
# how: make verify EL_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=360
# EL_READY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=180
# how: make verify PIPELINERUN_WAIT_SECONDS=300
# PIPELINERUN_WAIT_SECONDS=120
# Resilient HTTP GET (http_get_retry) — used for transient-prone internet
# fetches like the ArgoCD install manifest from raw.githubusercontent.com,
# which rate-limits (HTTP 429). Retries with exponential backoff.
# COMMENTED, like every other per-run knob: load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER the
# caller's environment, so an uncommented value here silently defeats `make X HTTP_GET_RETRIES=8`.
# The code-side defaults in lib/os.sh's http_get_retry are IDENTICAL (5 / 5 / 60), so commenting
# them changes no behaviour — it only makes an override work.
# how: make deps HTTP_GET_RETRIES=8
# HTTP_GET_RETRIES=5
# HTTP_GET_RETRY_DELAY_SECONDS=5
# how: make mirror-pull HTTP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=30
# HTTP_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=10
# The whole-transfer cap for http_get_retry. 60s suits the small manifests it was written for; the
# three LARGE pinned-CLI downloads use their own knob below instead.
# how: make deps HTTP_GET_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=120
# HTTP_GET_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=60
# Ceiling for one http_get_retry call. ⚠️ NOT a whole-call ceiling and do not describe it as one:
# it is checked BEFORE each attempt and BEFORE each sleep, never DURING a transfer, so the TRUE worst
# case is HTTP_GET_TOTAL_BUDGET_SECONDS + HTTP_GET_MAX_TIME_SECONDS (MEASURED: budget=10 max-time=30
# against a hanging server -> 30s elapsed, 3x the bound an earlier draft of this comment claimed).
# DEFAULT IS DERIVED: 5 x HTTP_GET_MAX_TIME_SECONDS, so a call site that widens one widens the other
# — 00-install-prereqs.sh raises max-time to 900 for the 238 MiB argocd download, and a FIXED 300s
# budget there made retries STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE (one attempt, then a die naming a knob the
# operator never set). 0 = unlimited, matching curl's own convention. A non-numeric value now DIES
# rather than silently disabling the guard. It exists because the helper used to hand the OUTER
# loop's DOUBLING delay to an INNER `curl --retry 3 --retry-delay`, and the two COMPOUNDED into
# 3x(5+10+20+40+80)+(5+10+20+40) = 540s of pure sleep against a connection refused in 0 ms
# (MEASURED 2026-08-17: 9m00.5s refused, 12m20.5s blackholed). The inner --retry is gone; this is the
# backstop so no future edit can rebuild that product silently.
# how: make deps HTTP_GET_TOTAL_BUDGET_SECONDS=600
# HTTP_GET_TOTAL_BUDGET_SECONDS=300
# Caps the exponential backoff so a failure lands sooner. It does NOT buy more attempts —
# the loop is bounded by HTTP_GET_RETRIES, not by the budget.
# how: make deps HTTP_GET_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_SECONDS=60
# HTTP_GET_RETRY_MAX_DELAY_SECONDS=30
# The three LARGE pinned-CLI downloads in 00-install-prereqs.sh (tkn, argocd 238 MiB, kubectl 57 MiB)
# use their OWN cap: 60s demands 33 Mbit/s sustained for argocd, and below that every attempt dies
# curl 28. COMMENTED on purpose — load_env sources this file with `set -a` AFTER a per-run override
# is in the environment, so an uncommented value here would silently defeat it (which is exactly what
# happened to the first version of this fix, caught by check-env-clobber).
# how: make deps PREREQ_DOWNLOAD_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=1800    # a very slow link
# PREREQ_DOWNLOAD_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=900
# The internet PRESENCE probe (require_internet), run once at the top of `make mirror-pull`. Distinct
# from the retries above, and deliberately impatient: those exist to survive a FLAKY network, which makes
# them agonisingly slow against an ABSENT one (measured: >2 min of retries on ONE url before failing with
# a curl error that named googleapis, not "you have no internet"). This probe HEADs storage.googleapis.com
# then github.com and fails in seconds, pointing at the sneakernet flow.
# Raise these only if a slow corporate proxy makes the probe a false negative.
# stage: you choose (optional)
# how: make mirror-pull INTERNET_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
# INTERNET_PROBE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# how: make mirror-pull INTERNET_PROBE_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=30
# INTERNET_PROBE_MAX_TIME_SECONDS=10
# CHECK_TOOLS_PHASE — which box is this, and where in the runbook?
#   (unset / full)  every tool must be present HERE, NOW. The default, and what `make preflight` uses.
#   pre-carry       the SNEAKERNET AIR-GAPPED jump box, BEFORE `make bundle-load`. The five CARRIED
#                   tools (crane/kubectl/helm/jq/yq) are EXPECTED to be missing — the bundle brings
#                   them — so their absence is not a failure. The OS packages the tarball CANNOT carry
#                   still are. docs/sneakernet.md passes this for you.
# COMMENTED, and it MUST stay commented: this is a per-run toggle, and `load_env` sources this file with
# `set -a` AFTER make puts an override in the environment — so an uncommented value here would silently
# WIN over `make check-tools CHECK_TOOLS_PHASE=pre-carry`. (See `make check-env-clobber`.)
# stage: you choose (a per-run flag, not a value to obtain)
# CHECK_TOOLS_PHASE=pre-carry
# How long `make check-tools` may spend asking one tool for its version. It exists because the probe
# was NOT offline: `kubectl --version` errors (unknown flag), so the old generic fallback ran
# `kubectl version`, which CONTACTS THE API SERVER — ~130s of silence on a box whose kubeconfig points
# at a firewalled Supervisor VIP, inside the gate we call "the cheapest failure available".
# how: make check-tools TOOL_VERSION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=15
# TOOL_VERSION_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# ALLOW_PUBLIC_CHARTS — let `make install-ingress` (default: istio) fetch its helm charts from the
# INTERNET even though a bundle is present but carries none. COMMENTED (a per-run escape hatch; an
# uncommented value here would be exported by load_env and silently defeat the refusal).
# By default that case is a HARD FAILURE: on a dual-homed box, silently fetching would turn a BROKEN
# BUNDLE into a green install that proves nothing about the air gap — and the air-gapped operator then
# discovers it on the one box that cannot fix it. Re-cut the bundle instead:
#   make mirror-pull && make bundle
# stage: you choose (a per-run flag, not a value to obtain)
# ALLOW_PUBLIC_CHARTS=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Pinned component versions (mirror of images/images.txt + .mise.toml)
#    Keep these aligned with images/images.txt; Renovate tracks the image tags.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=tektoncd/pipeline
TEKTON_PIPELINES_VERSION=v1.15.0
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=tektoncd/triggers
TEKTON_TRIGGERS_VERSION=v0.37.0
# Tekton Dashboard — the web UI for PipelineRuns/TaskRuns/logs, at TEKTON_DASHBOARD_HOST.
# Installed READ-ONLY (release.yaml; no mutation from the UI). The Dashboard has NO built-in
# auth — access is network/ingress-gated, so it needs no login (see .trivyignore rationale
# and the README "Access the UIs" note). Its image is mirrored from ghcr.io like the other
# Tekton controllers (derived from the release manifest, not images.txt).
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=tektoncd/dashboard
TEKTON_DASHBOARD_VERSION=v0.71.0
# The app RUNTIME image (eclipse-temurin JRE). Renovate-tracked in lockstep with
# images/images.txt; check-image-alignment fails CI if it drifts, and
# check-java-alignment fails if its major diverges from apps/java/javawebapp/pom.xml's java.version.
# (Only the JRE is mirrored — the in-cluster BUILD uses the maven:...-temurin image,
# so no standalone temurin JDK is needed.)
# renovate: datasource=docker depName=eclipse-temurin
TEMURIN_JRE_TAG=25.0.4_7-jre-jammy

# The .NET runtime base. A SEPARATE image from the SDK builder base (unlike python/nodejs, whose
# builder and runtime are the same image) -- the SDK is ~800 MB and the CHISELLED aspnet runtime has
# no shell and no package manager, which is what makes the runtime-stage no-install invariant free
# for this app rather than enforced. Mirrored in images/images.txt under its own # renovate:
# annotation; check-image-alignment fails CI if the two drift.
# renovate: datasource=docker depName=mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet
DOTNET_ASPNET_TAG=10.0-noble-chiseled

# --- Go app (gowebapp) base images -------------------------------------------------------------
# Keep BOTH aligned with images/images.txt — `make check-image-alignment` reads each app's
# Dockerfile ARGs straight from apps/registry.tsv and fails on drift (a mirrored-but-unreferenced
# tag means Kaniko asks Harbor for an image it never received: MANIFEST_UNKNOWN at pipeline time).
# renovate: datasource=docker depName=golang
GOLANG_BUILD_TAG=1.27.0-bookworm
# distroless publishes NO semver tags and `nonroot` MOVES, so it is pinned by DIGEST — the tag
# alone is not reproducible in an air-gap bundle. Renovate bumps the DIGEST (the customManager
# splits `nonroot` as currentValue from the @sha256 as currentDigest); do NOT add `versioning=`
# here — the docker datasource already defaults to docker versioning and the extra token does
# not match the manager's pattern, which would silently untrack this pin.
# renovate: datasource=docker depName=gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12
DISTROLESS_STATIC_TAG=nonroot@sha256:afa5c872c891853ca7fcf1f12c3edb23f7eeef36189728842dd51042ff57f7ab

# CLI tools not covered by mise (downloaded by scripts/00-install-prereqs.sh).
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=tektoncd/cli extractVersion=^v(?<version>.*)$
TKN_VERSION=0.46.0
# The argocd CLI SHOULD match the ArgoCD server (ARGOCD_VERSION) major line — same
# argoproj/argo-cd product. Keep this aligned with ARGOCD_VERSION below; Renovate
# tracks both (same depName) and bumps them together.
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=argoproj/argo-cd
ARGOCD_CLI_VERSION=v3.5.1
# kubectl fallback version (only used by 00-install-prereqs.sh if mise didn't
# provide it). Kept aligned with .mise.toml's kubectl pin by check-toolchain-alignment.
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=kubernetes/kubernetes
KUBECTL_VERSION=v1.36.4

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 10. KinD local end-to-end (simulates the "VKS-provided" Harbor + ArgoCD locally)
#     Used only by `make e2e-kind` / kind-* targets. On real VKS these are given.
#     The kind flow writes discovered values (HARBOR_URL, KUBECONFIG, ...) to a
#     gitignored .env.kind overlay that overrides the values above.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
KIND_CLUSTER_NAME=vks-airgap-cicd
# Empty = use the kind binary's built-in (version-locked) default node image.
KIND_NODE_IMAGE=
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=kubernetes-sigs/cloud-provider-kind
CLOUD_PROVIDER_KIND_VERSION=v0.11.1
# renovate: datasource=helm depName=harbor registryUrl=https://helm.goharbor.io
HARBOR_CHART_VERSION=1.19.2
HARBOR_NAMESPACE=harbor
# renovate: datasource=github-releases depName=argoproj/argo-cd
ARGOCD_VERSION=v3.5.1
# ArgoCD 'admin' UI password for the LOCAL KinD ArgoCD. KinD ONLY — on real VKS,
# ArgoCD is provided by the lab with a lab-set password, so leave this blank there.
# Optional: set it (in .env, like GITEA_ADMIN_PASSWORD / HARBOR_PASSWORD) for a known,
# stable login; the KinD install applies it. Leave blank/commented to keep ArgoCD's
# auto-generated password — either way `make argocd-password` / `make creds` reveal it
# (they self-resolve the kubeconfig, so no kube-context juggling). Commented so it stays
# unset by default and the generated-password fallback works.
# stage: you choose (login for the component we install / KinD only)
# ARGOCD_ADMIN_PASSWORD=<SET-IN-.env>

# ARGOCD_USERNAME — the ArgoCD login `make creds-show` reports.
#
# COMMENTED because the code defaults it to `admin`, and an uncommented value here would be re-sourced by
# load_env's `set -a` over a per-run override. Unset ⇒ `admin`.
#
# You only need this when you are NOT the ArgoCD admin — i.e. a TENANT (Scenario 2), where the platform
# team granted you a named user. If they gave you a TOKEN instead, set ARGOCD_AUTH_TOKEN and leave this
# alone: creds-show then reports `(token)` rather than inventing an `admin` login you do not have.
# ARGOCD_USERNAME=admin

# ---- The state overlay (DISCOVERED state: LB IPs, kubeconfig, generated passwords) ----------------
#
# Where the flow WRITES what it discovers. It used to be a hardcoded `.env.kind` — a KinD-named file
# that also carried REAL-LAB state, which is how `make kind-down` (Step 0 of BOTH real-lab runbooks)
# came to destroy an operator's lab kubeconfig and Gitea token.
#
# It is now STAMPED with the cluster that wrote it:
#   * `make state-show`   — which cluster owns this file, when it was written, what is in it
#   * `make kind-down`    — removes it ONLY if the KinD flow stamped it (VKS_STATE_KIND=1)
#   * a MISMATCHED stamp  — the file is ARCHIVED (renamed), never deleted: it may hold the only copy
#                           of a live cluster's generated passwords
#
# COMMENTED, and load-bearing: this SELECTS which sink you read and write. An uncommented value here
# would be exported by load_env's `set -a` and would defeat a per-run override (a second cluster, or
# a container harness, needs its own sink and must not leak into the repo's).
#   how: leave unset for the default (./.env.state); set it per-run for an isolated sink.
# VKS_STATE_FILE=./.env.state
